WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.460
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.420
Welcome to the Mapping
As Knowing Lecture Series

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:11.770
of the Franke Program

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:14.350
in science and the humanities at Yale.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:19.350
I am delighted to introduce
our speaker this evening.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.090
So, science fiction writer
Kim Stanley Robinson,

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.390
who will be talking to us about

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:29.620
mapping the future of the Anthropocene.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:31.690
So, first I want to...

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:34.430
I see Richard and Barbara Franke there

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.550
signed in to our Zoom universe.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:42.750
So, I wanted to thank both
of them for their generosity

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.570
in endowing the Franke Program,

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:48.500
that is allowing us to post
these wonderful events.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.040
So, thank you, Richard
and Barbara and welcome.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.690
I also want to remind
everyone who has signed in

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:58.180
that we are recording this.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.880
So, could you kindly turn off your videos

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.210
and mute your microphones for now?

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:08.770
And the format that we are
planning to have for this evening

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:12.310
is that Stan will be speaking
for about 40, 45 minutes

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:15.310
and we will take questions afterward.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.760
So, kindly type in your
questions on the chat

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:22.060
and Ty Kamp,

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:23.680
Hi, Ty, hello.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.720
She will be managing the chat.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.660
And I also want to give
a big shout out to Ty

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:29.940
without whose help

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.243
we will not be able to run
this program as smoothly.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.110
So, very grateful Ty
for your help with this.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:41.590
So, without further ado,

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:42.950
let me introduce,

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:44.750
I don't quite know where to start.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:47.270
So, first I have to control
my gushiness a little bit

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:52.270
because I am a really ardent
fan of Stan and Stan's writing.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.510
So, Stan, as you all know,

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:59.510
is one of the foremost writers
of the science fiction genre.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:07.350
And he began publishing novels in 1984.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.970
And his work has been described

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.490
as humanist science fiction,

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:14.370
literary science fiction.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.110
So, in a way he crosses genres,

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:22.000
although one could fairly comfortably say

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.700
that what he really envisions and imagines

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:27.440
are views of the future

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.000
but infused with an
emotional, psychological

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.180
and human touch.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:35.683
So, it's not just about
robots and automation.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:40.800
So, he is a very proud
defender and advocate

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.200
of the genre of science fiction.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.380
And he actually regards,

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:49.150
and I think me and many
other fans around the world

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:51.360
concur that it's one of the most powerful

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.980
of all literary forms,

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.890
because it allows us to imagine futures,

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:59.180
futures that we all dream about

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.803
and futures that we sometimes
are scared of as well.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.620
So, he was born in Illinois

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:08.080
but moved to California

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:09.190
when he was very young

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:12.510
and he has remained a loyal Californian.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:16.060
He has often talked about...

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:18.130
I want to give you a bit of a feel for him

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:19.900
because this is one of
the things about Zoom,

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.820
because you can't see
the person in the flesh.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:23.653
So, I want to give you

00:03:23.653 --> 00:03:25.840
a little more sort of
biographical information

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:29.890
so that you can kind of imagine
what must be influencing

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:32.890
the way in which he
thinks and sees the world.

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:35.720
So, he grew up playing in orange groves,

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.030
stretching out for miles

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:39.980
and in a very suburban part of California

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.670
that over his lifetime
fundamentally transformed.

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:47.670
And with encroaching
urbanism and modern life

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:51.490
kind of entering closer and closer in

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:54.660
and changing sort of the
environment around him.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:56.810
So, he was very sensitive to that

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:00.390
and I mention this because
many of you who read him

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:03.470
will see that this attunement to nature

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:04.780
and to the attunement

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.790
to how humans have been
transforming nature

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:10.450
is a very fundamental
theme that runs through

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:12.870
many of his books.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:17.210
So, he actually stumbled
upon science fiction late,

00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:18.290
relatively speaking,

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:20.140
while he was in college.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:22.950
And then it made such a strong impression

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:25.440
that he knew immediately
that he was really committed

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:26.810
to becoming a writer

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:28.140
and that was the genre

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:30.710
that he would really pick for himself.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:33.500
So, he enrolled at University
of California at San Diego

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:36.003
and received his BA in Literature.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:39.930
And he started at that time already

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.270
he had developed the idea
of writing a trio of books

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:45.490
exploring three different
alternative histories

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:47.337
in which Southern California
had gone down different paths.

00:04:47.337 --> 00:04:52.337
And this is the famous
"Orange County Trilogy."

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:54.750
Then he briefly left California

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.500
and came eastward to Boston University

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:58.740
and did an MA in English

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.710
and then went back to UCLA to finish PhD.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:05.150
So, he finished his PhD in 1982

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:07.040
and a revised version of his PhD

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.920
was what was published in '84

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:11.600
as the novels of Philip K. Dick.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.063
So, after publishing these few novels,

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:17.770
Stan moved with his wife,

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:21.210
who is an environmental
toxicologist and a renowned chemist,

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:22.450
and they moved to Switzerland

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:23.740
where they lived for a few years

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:26.290
and then he began at that
point to write full-time.

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:28.170
Then they came back to Washington DC.

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.530
But eventually they just
moved back to California

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:33.120
and he lives still in Davis

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:37.643
I think since 1991, right,
you've been in Davis.

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:43.090
And they actually live
with his wife and son

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:45.050
they live in Village Homes,

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:47.360
which is sort of a plant community

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:49.700
that shares many things in
common with the community

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:53.400
that he depicts in his
1999 old "Pacific Edge."

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:58.400
And so, he is actually a very
proud stay-at-home parent,

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:02.000
which gives them a lot of
time to write and think

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.390
and his wife who's career he has supported

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:07.470
works full-time as a chemist.

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:11.600
And many of you have likely read

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:13.560
his "Aurora," the "Mars Trilogy."

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:15.010
I don't know where to start.

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:17.530
And he has so many awards and recognition

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:21.760
that I'm not going to take any
more time from his talk today

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:24.690
to mention them.

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:26.060
So, what can I say?

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:28.507
I'm personally an ardent fan of Stan

00:06:28.507 --> 00:06:29.952
and as a scientist

00:06:29.952 --> 00:06:31.670
and as an astrophysicist,

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:35.570
I'm very finicky about the
sci-fi that I read and love.

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:37.410
I find that it's often very hard

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:39.140
to get that needed distance

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:42.200
to derive pleasure from the writing

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:45.170
as when as always apt to
interrogate the science

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:46.430
all the time.

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:47.645
And so, Stan's writing is one

00:06:47.645 --> 00:06:50.400
that I have had no trouble with at all.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.510
And his books have been singular

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:55.270
as while they don't always necessarily

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:58.210
take me where I intended to go,

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:02.530
I have always ended up
where I needed to be.

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:07.400
So, I would like to turn it over to Stan

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:09.120
and we really look forward to,

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.820
and thank you so much
for agreeing to speak

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:13.653
and we look forward to hearing you.

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:16.810
- Well, thank you very much

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:18.690
for that kind of introduction, Priya,

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:20.210
and thank you, Ty

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:23.460
and the Frankes and Yale.

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:24.900
It's a real pleasure,

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:29.460
and I hope that we also get
to do this in person someday

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:30.620
post vaccine

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:34.310
in a more mobile and social time.

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:36.490
I really look forward to that.

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:37.323
And yes,

00:07:37.323 --> 00:07:40.980
today I'm gonna talk about
mapping the Anthropocene.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:42.790
And it's an interesting assignment

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:46.010
kind of given me by Priya
and by this speaking series

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:47.840
and allowed me to think about it

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:50.223
in ways that I quite enjoyed.

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:54.910
If you talk about mapping the future

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:58.980
then you are necessarily
talking about cognitive mapping.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:03.140
So, that's really the framework
for everything then follows.

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:05.270
And so, as Priya said,

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:07.500
I'll speak for about 40 to 45 minutes

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:10.780
and then hope to engage
with questions as well.

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:12.290
So, cognitive mapping.

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:15.590
This is a phrase that my
teacher Fredric Jameson,

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:17.893
who also taught at Yale
for a couple of years,

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:22.490
brings forth is it's somewhat
like weather forecasting

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:24.090
or a modeling exercise

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:27.410
or scenario building trajectory following.

00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:29.320
You see it a lot in the sciences

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:32.620
and everywhere else.

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:35.670
And it's necessarily ideological.

00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:37.820
And I mean that in the best sense

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:42.110
because I take ideology to
mean an imaginary relationship

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:44.210
to the real situation.

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:45.780
I like this definition a lot

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:50.090
because there is a real
situation out there

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:53.750
but our knowledge of
it has to be imaginary

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:56.410
and by which I mean selective,

00:08:56.410 --> 00:09:00.280
partial and shaped by
our prior assumptions

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:04.000
so that we see some
evidence better than others

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.070
based on our axioms.

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:11.070
And so, maps become a kind of
an analogy for this process

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:13.580
and maps to our ideological.

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:15.380
And by analogy,

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:18.110
think of the Mercator projection,

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:22.140
where Greenland is as
large as North America

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:26.270
and Novaya Zemlya is
as large as Australia.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:28.060
This is a distortion

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:30.760
that is made by some axioms

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:35.140
that you use to put the
globe onto a flat surface.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:37.050
Now you can try to adjust for that

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:40.220
and this is exactly what ideological work

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:42.750
can be conceptualized as.

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:44.210
You can make adjustments

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:46.400
to keep the continent sized accurately

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:48.010
to the way that they
are in the real world.

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:50.730
You have to cut a globe apart

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:55.700
and spread out your map in a
kind of transverse directions,

00:09:55.700 --> 00:10:00.410
kind of a giant triangle on a wall.

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:02.950
And this, you get a different distortion,

00:10:02.950 --> 00:10:06.840
which is that the oceans are erased

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:09.770
and Antarctica doesn't
get included very well.

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:10.810
So, in the end,

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:15.220
you might even want a
globe instead of a map.

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:16.930
And of course, this is
another kind of map,

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:19.600
but it's cast onto the same shape

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:22.210
as the thing it's trying to represent.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:24.880
And this might be an
interesting way to think of.

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:28.090
Can you adjust your ideology
to get a better view

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:30.160
of what you're looking at?

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:34.390
And also, if you're
mapping the Anthropocene,

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:35.740
you're mapping the future.

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:38.970
You're mapping time rather than space.

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:41.890
And here it gets incredibly complicated

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:45.313
because what unit of time is relevant?

00:10:46.489 --> 00:10:50.170
This would be something
to talk with Priya about

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:51.003
in more detail

00:10:51.003 --> 00:10:53.670
but it seems to me that I hear
that in subatomic physics,

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:57.050
there is indeed a minimum unit of time

00:10:57.050 --> 00:10:59.150
that you can't go below

00:10:59.150 --> 00:11:04.150
because I think it's the
Pauli exclusion principle

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:08.440
that objects can't occupy the same space.

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:11.910
And then the speed of
light crossing that space

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:12.920
gets you to something like

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:16.740
10 to the negative 34th of a second.

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:18.420
That's a pretty short minimum,

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:19.990
but you can't go smaller

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:22.360
without breaking the laws of physics.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:25.430
And then at the other
end you have eternity

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:27.280
or maybe the age of the universe.

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:29.330
13.82 billion years

00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:31.580
is the latest estimates of astrophysicists

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:34.093
and I love that too at
the end of the 0.82.

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:39.563
That's pretty precise considering
the question involves.

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:42.130
But in between that,

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:43.870
if you're trying to map the future,

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:46.285
what do you do?

00:11:46.285 --> 00:11:49.300
And can you look at your
assumptions in a way that,

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:50.133
if for instance

00:11:50.133 --> 00:11:52.130
going back to maps of the world,

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:54.590
that puts South at the top?

00:11:54.590 --> 00:11:57.680
And I think that's a good
thing to keep in mind

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:00.620
that there was probably
assumptions that we're making

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:04.920
that we seldom interrogate or think about.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:06.900
Why is North always at the top of the map

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:07.810
and South of the bottom?

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:08.643
When you reverse it,

00:12:08.643 --> 00:12:10.090
it looks weird and wrong,

00:12:10.090 --> 00:12:11.500
but maybe you need to do that

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:15.510
to realize that it's arbitrary
and sort of axiomatic.

00:12:15.510 --> 00:12:19.163
So, let's think about the Anthropocene.

00:12:21.380 --> 00:12:22.790
What is it?

00:12:22.790 --> 00:12:25.720
Well, it's another cognitive mapping

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:29.750
and it's of splitting up time into units.

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:32.910
So, some unit between
the minimum and eternity.

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:36.980
And this is what historians
would call periodization.

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:39.870
And again, my teacher, Fred Jameson,

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.650
we cannot not periodize.

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:43.483
In other words,

00:12:43.483 --> 00:12:45.420
you can never look at history and say,

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:47.310
well, it's just one uninterrupted flow

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:49.790
from the beginning of the
universe till right now

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:51.860
and you just take it one second at a time.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:53.040
We don't do that.

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:54.430
We periodize.

00:12:54.430 --> 00:12:57.720
And each system of periodization

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:02.270
reveals a theory of history
that is making the cuts.

00:13:02.270 --> 00:13:04.270
So, you can simply do it numerically.

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:05.540
There's the 16th century,

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:06.420
the 17th century,

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:08.580
the 18th century, the 19th century.

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:10.670
Numerical, and it's simple in that regard,

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:11.980
but that's still periodizing

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:12.900
then in each century

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:15.200
you think something different is going on.

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:17.430
There's systems of power.

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:20.120
Imperial systems like
the Chinese dynasties.

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:23.410
That's how they periodized
Chinese history sometimes.

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:24.490
Or empire names.

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:28.140
The Roman empire, the
Persian empire, and so on.

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:29.870
There's the technological.

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:31.580
So, you have the paleolithic

00:13:31.580 --> 00:13:33.590
followed by the agricultural revolution,

00:13:33.590 --> 00:13:35.220
the industrial revolution,

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:37.173
steam era, coal era,

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:39.240
the electronic era,

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:40.810
the nuclear era,

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:41.840
the digital era.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:45.020
These are all technological
periodizations.

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:46.970
And then you have the cultural.

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:48.660
So, middle ages,

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:49.570
the Renaissance,

00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:50.900
the enlightenment,

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:52.350
the modern period,

00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:53.660
the postmodern period,

00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:58.300
which we were in until this
word Anthropocene got invented,

00:13:58.300 --> 00:13:59.920
but nobody was particularly happy

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:02.770
because what comes after post modernism?

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:04.700
You would have to have post postmodernism.

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:07.000
And it was clearly a provisional name,

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.410
but something was changing in modernism

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:12.610
that hadn't yet been properly theorized.

00:14:12.610 --> 00:14:15.340
So it was a tag name that
started in the mid '80s,

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:17.570
again with Jameson's strong support.

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:20.550
And now I think has been superseded

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:21.780
by the name Anthropocene

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:23.520
but we'll talk about that.

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:24.353
And then lastly,

00:14:24.353 --> 00:14:26.120
there's the geological.

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:28.210
Okay, the fossil record

00:14:28.210 --> 00:14:30.700
gives us a way of interpreting

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:32.250
the past of the earth,

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:35.420
such that there are break
points along the way

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:37.090
and names that go along with them.

00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:38.710
And this is really complicated.

00:14:38.710 --> 00:14:41.570
There is actually a
stratigraphy committee,

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:43.710
the American Geophysical Union

00:14:43.710 --> 00:14:48.370
and geologists in general have
parsed the past very finely

00:14:48.370 --> 00:14:50.470
in different formats of changes.

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:52.750
But let's just think about the Triassic,

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:54.891
the Jurassic, the Cretaceous

00:14:54.891 --> 00:14:56.720
and then after the Cretaceous,

00:14:56.720 --> 00:14:58.680
which ended 65 million years ago,

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:00.660
which with what we now consider

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:04.373
to probably be a meteor
impact at the KT boundary.

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:10.100
Subsequent post Cretaceous,
also divided into periods,

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:12.620
that eventually ended up in the Holocene,

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:14.380
which was where we've been

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:18.380
since the end of the last ice
age about 11,000 years ago.

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:20.670
Okay, and all of that,

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:22.680
the Anthropocene is a word invented

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:26.120
by a Eugene steamer in the 1980s.

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:30.140
And it was picked up by
scientist Paul Crewdson

00:15:30.140 --> 00:15:32.250
in 2000 or 2002.

00:15:32.250 --> 00:15:35.810
He was using this
periodization politically,

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:37.800
and this is so often the case,

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:41.630
in order to emphasize the
danger of human impacts

00:15:41.630 --> 00:15:42.933
on earth's biosystem.

00:15:45.830 --> 00:15:46.960
He was trying to make the point

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:50.350
that humanity had become the largest force

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:51.640
in the critical zone

00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:55.740
from the top of the atmosphere
to the bottom of the bed,

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:57.060
to bedrock,

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:58.690
the live stone, you might say.

00:15:58.690 --> 00:15:59.930
The biosphere itself.

00:15:59.930 --> 00:16:02.725
That humans were the largest force

00:16:02.725 --> 00:16:06.250
in transforming that zone quickly

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:08.950
and mostly in a bad way

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:12.160
and that was why you would
call it the Anthropocene.

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:16.280
And this definition is
therefore a combination

00:16:16.280 --> 00:16:20.470
of the geological kind of cut,

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:22.180
the human various cuts

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:24.210
that sometimes get called big history

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:25.863
so that you include the planet,

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:29.220
but also the other ones culturally

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:31.680
and all other periodization schemes

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:35.030
have collided in this
term, the Anthropocene

00:16:35.030 --> 00:16:38.940
to give it a particular
power and suggestiveness

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:43.930
to our reckoning of where
we are now in history.

00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:45.430
Now, I wanna note on the side

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:48.130
that the Anthropocene,
as such, as a definition,

00:16:48.130 --> 00:16:50.410
is a kind of a science fiction story.

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:53.650
If you imagine that 25
million years in the future,

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:55.070
aliens come to earth,

00:16:55.070 --> 00:16:57.170
alien geologists or scientists

00:16:57.170 --> 00:16:59.200
or some post-human version of us

00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:00.500
come back to earth or whatever

00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:01.650
and they look at it

00:17:01.650 --> 00:17:03.840
and they study its geological record,

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:06.290
they will be able to see a change point

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:08.320
like the KT boundary

00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:12.820
that marks where humans began
to intervene so intensively

00:17:13.840 --> 00:17:16.210
that it could be seen
25 million years later

00:17:16.210 --> 00:17:18.670
in the geological record
of just the rocks.

00:17:18.670 --> 00:17:21.810
Well, that's a provocation.

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:23.670
Immediately, people ask

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:26.040
well, what marker are you talking about?

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:28.180
I think this question
is not so interesting

00:17:28.180 --> 00:17:31.530
because if you add up all the markers,

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:34.310
it will be undeniably the case.

00:17:34.310 --> 00:17:36.980
It could be the rapid shift in CO2 levels,

00:17:36.980 --> 00:17:38.887
although the aliens might say,

00:17:38.887 --> 00:17:42.320
"Well, something happened naturally."

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:45.380
It could be the sudden appearance
of radioactive elements

00:17:45.380 --> 00:17:48.200
in the surface from our atomic explosions.

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:50.740
It could be a layer of
plastics and micro plastics,

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:53.960
although will those last 25 million years?

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:55.320
Et cetera, et cetera.

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:58.410
The Anthropocene as where it started

00:17:58.410 --> 00:18:02.610
has become a kind of minor
academic argument or game

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:03.443
that people play

00:18:03.443 --> 00:18:07.050
to make various points
about the impacts of humans.

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:09.430
When Europeans arrived in the new world,

00:18:09.430 --> 00:18:11.520
their diseases and their genocide

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:13.180
killed off the local populations

00:18:13.180 --> 00:18:15.270
to the point that the areas of land

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:17.430
that had been being kept clear

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:21.240
by new world native American populations

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:22.440
reforested so quickly

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:25.910
that there was a drop in the
CO2 level in the atmosphere

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:28.960
at that time, et cetera.

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:31.070
Let's say that probably

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:32.530
the atomic explosions

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:35.620
will serve like the iridium
layer at the KT boundary.

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:38.050
That it's weird and there
was no other good explanation

00:18:38.050 --> 00:18:41.110
so that the Anthropocene is probably real.

00:18:41.110 --> 00:18:43.820
Now, another game in academia

00:18:43.820 --> 00:18:46.360
immediately came into play.

00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:49.990
Maybe it's not humanities
impact or the Anthropocene.

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:52.990
Maybe it's the way we've
been exploiting the earth

00:18:52.990 --> 00:18:54.020
and other people

00:18:54.020 --> 00:18:56.120
that has made an irrevocable mark

00:18:56.120 --> 00:18:57.580
in the geological record.

00:18:57.580 --> 00:19:02.100
And so, it's been called
provisionally the Capitalocene,

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:04.540
suggesting that it wasn't
people but capitalism

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:06.470
that left the big marks

00:19:06.470 --> 00:19:08.060
in the geological record.

00:19:08.060 --> 00:19:09.920
But now this is another
kind of science fiction

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:11.653
called the alternative history.

00:19:12.664 --> 00:19:13.497
In other words,

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:15.380
you're implying that

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:18.870
if there had been a different
social organization for humans

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:22.970
since the 16th century,

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:24.760
since modernization,

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:28.950
that would have not left the
same kind of mark on the earth.

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:32.570
You could imagine a sociolo scene

00:19:32.570 --> 00:19:34.530
or a medicino scene

00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:36.110
or a scienco scene

00:19:36.110 --> 00:19:38.070
or a utopiao scene

00:19:38.070 --> 00:19:41.410
in which the geological
mark wouldn't be clear

00:19:41.410 --> 00:19:43.580
because we had done such a good job

00:19:43.580 --> 00:19:48.580
of matching our activities to
the biosphere's capacities.

00:19:48.930 --> 00:19:51.910
Well, I think this is very unlikely

00:19:51.910 --> 00:19:53.430
and alternative history is weak

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:55.630
precisely because you can't test it.

00:19:55.630 --> 00:19:56.983
It's just an assertion.

00:19:58.625 --> 00:20:00.900
And therefore it's impossible to prove.

00:20:00.900 --> 00:20:03.870
And so, I think it's best
to stick to the Anthropocene

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:05.390
as the name for this.

00:20:05.390 --> 00:20:07.130
That, I think, can be defended

00:20:07.130 --> 00:20:10.210
as a real mark in geological history.

00:20:10.210 --> 00:20:12.930
So, what does it imply?

00:20:12.930 --> 00:20:14.630
Because I wanna say immediately,

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:18.610
and this is fundamental to
mapping the Anthropocene.

00:20:18.610 --> 00:20:22.740
The humanities impact on the biosphere

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:27.130
is not necessarily the
equivalent of saying disaster,

00:20:27.130 --> 00:20:31.840
of saying mass extinction
event and total disruption.

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:32.950
In other words,

00:20:32.950 --> 00:20:35.290
there could be a good Anthropocene.

00:20:35.290 --> 00:20:37.710
The Anthropocene could be a good one

00:20:37.710 --> 00:20:39.340
or it could be a bad one

00:20:39.340 --> 00:20:41.700
and that's the first part of our mapping.

00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:44.030
The first map of the Anthropocene

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:49.030
looks like a sort of a perfect spread out,

00:20:49.660 --> 00:20:51.620
a blunderbuss, you might say,

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:55.130
from one point with a
trajectory shooting off

00:20:55.130 --> 00:21:00.050
in all directions from a
really horrific catastrophe

00:21:00.050 --> 00:21:01.650
of a mass extinction event

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:04.093
that would hammer humanity as well.

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:07.050
But what I wanna say is that

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:08.810
this spread of possibilities right now,

00:21:08.810 --> 00:21:10.090
which is disorienting,

00:21:10.090 --> 00:21:11.810
and that's a good word to use

00:21:11.810 --> 00:21:13.810
when you're talking about maps.

00:21:13.810 --> 00:21:15.470
If the future that we're facing

00:21:15.470 --> 00:21:19.440
could be anything from our
horrific mass extinction event

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:21.460
that wrecks human civilization

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:25.520
and leaves us in some kind
of post-apocalyptic dystopia.

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:27.850
And yet on the other hand,

00:21:27.850 --> 00:21:31.860
it could also be a
progressively better adjustment

00:21:31.860 --> 00:21:33.370
to the biospheric limits

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:35.680
and to technologies and to social systems

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:39.270
such that we get to a prosperous age.

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:40.580
I don't wanna say utopia.

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:42.360
I don't wanna say golden age.

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:45.010
I just wanna say it could get really good.

00:21:45.010 --> 00:21:48.370
So, that being the case,

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:50.110
it's so confusing.

00:21:50.110 --> 00:21:53.750
But what I wanna suggest
is that from this point,

00:21:53.750 --> 00:21:55.960
if things begin to degrade

00:21:55.960 --> 00:21:59.340
and we don't address
our problems right away,

00:21:59.340 --> 00:22:00.840
then what you get to

00:22:06.290 --> 00:22:08.290
you begin to slide off into bad futures

00:22:08.290 --> 00:22:10.850
that are harder and harder to deal with.

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:13.280
If we deal with our problems now

00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:15.220
and begin to improve our systems

00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:16.990
and get a handle on our problems,

00:22:16.990 --> 00:22:18.890
then things begin to get better and better

00:22:18.890 --> 00:22:21.090
in ways that will be robust.

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:22.340
And what I wanna suggest is

00:22:22.340 --> 00:22:24.440
it's the middle that is excluded

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:26.933
in a kind of an Aristotelian way.

00:22:29.230 --> 00:22:32.240
It's as if we're walking down a peninsula

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:34.430
and this peninsula

00:22:34.430 --> 00:22:38.790
is a sharp on both
directions going downhill

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:41.190
and the peninsula is also attenuating

00:22:41.190 --> 00:22:42.460
and getting more and more narrow

00:22:42.460 --> 00:22:43.780
where choices have to be made.

00:22:43.780 --> 00:22:45.970
This is a kind of a backpacker's,

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:49.060
a rambler in the Sierras type image,

00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:49.893
but I bring it up

00:22:49.893 --> 00:22:51.497
because I wanna suggest that

00:22:51.497 --> 00:22:55.240
the moves we make now
will be determinative

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:57.100
for the next three or four generations

00:22:57.100 --> 00:23:00.230
of how desperate, if we were
to go down the bad side,

00:23:00.230 --> 00:23:01.660
how desperate it would be for them

00:23:01.660 --> 00:23:03.780
to crawl back over the
ridge onto the good side,

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:08.540
whereas right now it would
be less difficult to do it.

00:23:08.540 --> 00:23:12.200
Now, this map of the ridge edge

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:13.970
dropping to bad or good

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:17.790
is actually topologically
exactly the reverse

00:23:17.790 --> 00:23:20.670
of some maps I've seen of
our possibilities right now.

00:23:20.670 --> 00:23:23.440
And these are also valuable.

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:27.090
So, these are all thought exercises.

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:28.530
I'm thinking of a paper

00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:30.790
in the National Academy of Sciences

00:23:30.790 --> 00:23:32.070
that has the future

00:23:32.070 --> 00:23:35.520
as a kind of a big valley going downhill,

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:38.090
U-shaped Valley lichen and glacial area,

00:23:38.090 --> 00:23:40.070
where almost all of the futures

00:23:40.070 --> 00:23:43.280
lead to temperatures that are too high.

00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:46.493
And then if we do a certain
number of things correctly,

00:23:47.920 --> 00:23:52.920
you could go up and sideways
into a zone of safety.

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:54.480
And so, again,

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:57.520
it's topological or
topographical and it's imagery.

00:23:57.520 --> 00:23:58.890
It's like a map

00:23:58.890 --> 00:24:01.430
and it's the opposite of
the map I just suggested

00:24:01.430 --> 00:24:04.660
and yet it probably
speaks to something real

00:24:04.660 --> 00:24:08.540
that is maybe focused in
particular on our CO2 use.

00:24:08.540 --> 00:24:09.700
So, it's not wrong

00:24:09.700 --> 00:24:11.800
but these are three different
maps, right, in a row.

00:24:11.800 --> 00:24:14.210
The blunderbuss of the future

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:16.770
could be anything from terrible to gray.

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:20.740
The ridge going downhill suggesting that

00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:23.840
we best get it right now

00:24:24.740 --> 00:24:27.700
in order to make things better
for following generations.

00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:29.510
And then also the big Valley drop,

00:24:29.510 --> 00:24:32.340
which also suggests that that side canyon

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:33.550
is coming up quickly.

00:24:33.550 --> 00:24:35.683
That gets us off into a safe future.

00:24:36.610 --> 00:24:38.810
So, now for the rest of the talk here,

00:24:38.810 --> 00:24:43.660
I am going to ignore describing
the bad Anthropocene,

00:24:43.660 --> 00:24:45.613
because I think it's just too obvious.

00:24:47.260 --> 00:24:48.550
Essentially the name for it

00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:51.470
could be the mass extinction
event that we're heading into.

00:24:51.470 --> 00:24:54.710
And a mass extinction event
could lead to crop failures,

00:24:54.710 --> 00:24:58.000
which would lead to food
shortages for humans.

00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:00.230
It also could lead to
rises in temperatures

00:25:00.230 --> 00:25:03.290
to where you get up into
the wet-bulb 35 zone,

00:25:03.290 --> 00:25:07.950
which is that zone of heat
and humidity in combination

00:25:07.950 --> 00:25:11.800
that would lead to human deaths

00:25:11.800 --> 00:25:13.310
without air conditioning.

00:25:13.310 --> 00:25:14.143
In other words,

00:25:14.143 --> 00:25:15.940
overwhelming the human body's ability

00:25:15.940 --> 00:25:18.890
to regulate its temperatures,

00:25:18.890 --> 00:25:21.290
sweating doesn't work in
the high heat humidity.

00:25:21.290 --> 00:25:22.190
The heat gets high enough.

00:25:22.190 --> 00:25:25.190
The combination of the two
is a kind of heat index.

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:28.360
What bulb 35 is a fatal to human beings.

00:25:28.360 --> 00:25:32.100
And it could be only 95
degrees in terms of heat

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:33.940
but if that 95 degrees is combined

00:25:33.940 --> 00:25:35.250
with a super high humidity,

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:36.930
it becomes fatal.

00:25:36.930 --> 00:25:40.030
And so, civilization would be hammered

00:25:40.030 --> 00:25:42.170
in a bad Anthropocene.

00:25:42.170 --> 00:25:44.820
And that would mean that

00:25:44.820 --> 00:25:47.510
although humans probably
wouldn't go extinct

00:25:47.510 --> 00:25:51.600
because of a certain clever
resilience that we have

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:53.090
and there's so many of us

00:25:53.090 --> 00:25:57.010
that with the help of
our social organization

00:25:57.010 --> 00:25:59.730
and technology and hanging in there,

00:25:59.730 --> 00:26:04.170
it isn't really right
to talk about extinction

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:06.690
but it would certainly
be a post-traumatic time

00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:07.670
like, oh my gosh.

00:26:07.670 --> 00:26:08.770
In generations past,

00:26:08.770 --> 00:26:09.740
we screwed up so badly

00:26:09.740 --> 00:26:11.700
that we don't have any federal animals

00:26:11.700 --> 00:26:14.760
and we're only one 10th
the population we were.

00:26:14.760 --> 00:26:16.520
It would be a dark ages

00:26:16.520 --> 00:26:20.723
followed by a post-traumatic
and dysfunctional human time.

00:26:24.770 --> 00:26:27.220
It would make the dark
ages after the Roman empire

00:26:27.220 --> 00:26:30.380
look like almost nothing, really.

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:32.900
It would be way, way, way, way worse.

00:26:32.900 --> 00:26:34.730
So, let's leave that on the side.

00:26:34.730 --> 00:26:37.760
What would a good Anthropocene look like

00:26:37.760 --> 00:26:39.250
and how do we get there?

00:26:39.250 --> 00:26:41.330
So, good Anthropocene.

00:26:41.330 --> 00:26:43.206
Let's think about that.

00:26:43.206 --> 00:26:44.039
What it would look like

00:26:44.039 --> 00:26:45.190
I think is very simple.

00:26:45.190 --> 00:26:48.460
First of all, in a negative
sense, no mass extinction event.

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:50.750
We would have dodged the
mass extinction event

00:26:50.750 --> 00:26:55.470
and the other animals and
plants that are all endangered

00:26:55.470 --> 00:26:57.840
would still be with us
in a viable biosphere.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:26:59.380
Now that's a minimum,

00:26:59.380 --> 00:27:00.630
but it's very important

00:27:00.630 --> 00:27:03.920
because the only unfixable
thing in our biosphere

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:07.560
with the resilience and
robustness of life is extinction.

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:09.910
Don't be fooled by this
science fiction trope

00:27:09.910 --> 00:27:11.313
of de-extinction.

00:27:12.190 --> 00:27:13.420
It's interesting to try it

00:27:13.420 --> 00:27:17.390
and it might even work for a
couple of targeted species.

00:27:17.390 --> 00:27:20.610
It's a nice thought experiment
but it isn't a solution.

00:27:20.610 --> 00:27:23.620
It won't work for the
most of the extinctions

00:27:23.620 --> 00:27:24.940
that will happen.

00:27:24.940 --> 00:27:27.630
So, we need to Dodge a
mass extinction event.

00:27:27.630 --> 00:27:29.410
That's the minimum.

00:27:29.410 --> 00:27:32.170
Then also we have to think about people.

00:27:32.170 --> 00:27:33.130
It's our species.

00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:34.510
We're in the Anthropocene.

00:27:34.510 --> 00:27:36.100
What do humans do?

00:27:36.100 --> 00:27:37.480
You would want to postulate

00:27:37.480 --> 00:27:40.190
that there's enough food,
water, shelter, clothing,

00:27:40.190 --> 00:27:43.200
healthcare, education, work and security,

00:27:43.200 --> 00:27:46.290
safety, and peace for everybody.

00:27:46.290 --> 00:27:48.010
Adequacy for everybody.

00:27:48.010 --> 00:27:49.383
Nobody immiserated.

00:27:50.300 --> 00:27:53.870
That would be another benchmark
of a good Anthropocene.

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:56.210
And you can also just call that justice.

00:27:56.210 --> 00:27:59.440
So, a good Anthropocene
there would be justice.

00:27:59.440 --> 00:28:02.680
And then also in balance with
the biosphere's energy flows

00:28:02.680 --> 00:28:05.530
and with its ability to process

00:28:05.530 --> 00:28:08.000
our wastes and our pollution.

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:11.730
These are two very
important flow questions

00:28:11.730 --> 00:28:13.730
that not only humans

00:28:13.730 --> 00:28:15.440
would be in balanced with its biosphere

00:28:15.440 --> 00:28:17.350
but the immediate implication of that

00:28:17.350 --> 00:28:19.480
would be that all the
rest of the living world

00:28:19.480 --> 00:28:22.640
would also be at adequacy and okay.

00:28:22.640 --> 00:28:25.460
So, let's just say 10 billion people

00:28:25.460 --> 00:28:26.790
and hope that that's a maximum

00:28:26.790 --> 00:28:28.823
and it seems quite likely actually.

00:28:29.810 --> 00:28:31.950
And all of them with enough

00:28:31.950 --> 00:28:36.720
and all of the rest of the
living world has enough also.

00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:38.930
Now, can this be done?

00:28:38.930 --> 00:28:42.433
Physically in terms of energy flows, yes.

00:28:43.430 --> 00:28:45.770
We have the physical capability

00:28:45.770 --> 00:28:49.260
and the technological
capability of achieving that,

00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:50.820
which is good news, I think.

00:28:50.820 --> 00:28:52.730
And it comes out of an assessment

00:28:52.730 --> 00:28:57.330
of the scientific
communities investigations.

00:28:57.330 --> 00:29:00.060
It's only possible to get to that

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:03.200
if that becomes humanities project.

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:04.920
We won't kludge to it.

00:29:04.920 --> 00:29:07.180
We won't get there accidentally

00:29:07.180 --> 00:29:08.770
and we won't get there

00:29:08.770 --> 00:29:11.900
if we pursue humanities project right now,

00:29:11.900 --> 00:29:16.120
which is the creation of profit
in the capitalist system.

00:29:16.120 --> 00:29:18.460
That's an index

00:29:18.460 --> 00:29:22.590
that is putting together
any number of factors

00:29:22.590 --> 00:29:26.300
to make a number that accrues value

00:29:26.300 --> 00:29:30.360
to a small percentage of
the world's population

00:29:30.360 --> 00:29:31.840
in a power system.

00:29:31.840 --> 00:29:33.540
This is what capitalism is

00:29:33.540 --> 00:29:36.270
but the index of profit is fate

00:29:36.270 --> 00:29:38.300
on any number of levels

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:41.110
and it ends up being a
kind of a Ponzi scheme

00:29:41.110 --> 00:29:43.360
or a multi-generational Ponzi scheme.

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:45.860
So, this is another way to map the future.

00:29:45.860 --> 00:29:49.190
Systematically stealing in a kleptocracy

00:29:49.190 --> 00:29:50.640
from the generations to come

00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:53.210
as well as the rest of the life world.

00:29:53.210 --> 00:29:57.120
So, if we were gonna get
to a good Anthropocene,

00:29:57.120 --> 00:30:00.580
we would have to change
the political economy

00:30:00.580 --> 00:30:01.450
that we live in

00:30:01.450 --> 00:30:05.650
that we now are call
neoliberal late capitalism.

00:30:05.650 --> 00:30:08.740
The system of laws that
runs the world right now

00:30:08.740 --> 00:30:09.890
in all of our activities

00:30:09.890 --> 00:30:11.650
in an over determining way

00:30:11.650 --> 00:30:14.750
and even science itself has to get funded.

00:30:14.750 --> 00:30:19.750
This world is set up
with goals and rewards

00:30:20.680 --> 00:30:24.680
that are destroying the biosphere

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:27.630
and also wrecking human lives.

00:30:27.630 --> 00:30:30.470
This is the sad news, the bad news.

00:30:30.470 --> 00:30:32.490
It's as obvious as heck though.

00:30:32.490 --> 00:30:34.520
There's no reason for me to insist on it

00:30:34.520 --> 00:30:36.210
because if it weren't true,

00:30:36.210 --> 00:30:38.630
then why would we be in
the world that we're in?

00:30:38.630 --> 00:30:40.510
And of course there
have been improvements.

00:30:40.510 --> 00:30:42.200
There has been poverty reduction.

00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:44.000
There are good things happening.

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:48.100
They all happen as a gift
economy on this side.

00:30:48.100 --> 00:30:50.070
None of them have been profitable.

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:52.600
UN's millennial project of 2000

00:30:52.600 --> 00:30:57.600
to lift the poorest and
most immiserated people

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:01.090
on the planet up into adequacy,

00:31:01.090 --> 00:31:03.580
that's been achieved and
it wasn't even that costly

00:31:03.580 --> 00:31:04.880
but it wasn't profitable.

00:31:04.880 --> 00:31:06.110
And it was done on the side,

00:31:06.110 --> 00:31:08.300
outside the rule of profit.

00:31:08.300 --> 00:31:09.750
And that's interesting

00:31:09.750 --> 00:31:12.200
because if we could do that on the margin,

00:31:12.200 --> 00:31:13.250
what could we do

00:31:13.250 --> 00:31:16.493
if it was actually the whole
point of civilizations efforts?

00:31:18.110 --> 00:31:23.040
So, I'm gonna briefly
through some other points

00:31:23.040 --> 00:31:24.440
in a good Anthropocene.

00:31:24.440 --> 00:31:25.830
One of the necessary,

00:31:25.830 --> 00:31:27.130
I mean, it wouldn't be sufficient

00:31:27.130 --> 00:31:28.040
but it's necessary

00:31:28.040 --> 00:31:29.460
is to decarbonize fast

00:31:29.460 --> 00:31:31.380
and maybe even get into carbon draw-down

00:31:31.380 --> 00:31:32.810
out of the atmosphere.

00:31:32.810 --> 00:31:34.970
So, okay, on the technological level,

00:31:34.970 --> 00:31:39.190
we'd have the means to create
a steady state balanced

00:31:39.190 --> 00:31:41.490
with the biosphere but
we're not there yet.

00:31:41.490 --> 00:31:43.640
We've invented it but
we haven't applied it.

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:45.770
And right now the burning of fossil fuels

00:31:45.770 --> 00:31:49.110
is a major impact on the environment.

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:50.240
It'd be best to get back to

00:31:50.240 --> 00:31:53.210
350 parts per million
of CO2 in the atmosphere

00:31:53.210 --> 00:31:55.190
and there are various means to do that.

00:31:55.190 --> 00:31:57.570
Well, technological and biological,

00:31:57.570 --> 00:31:58.650
it may be could be done

00:31:58.650 --> 00:32:00.910
but it would be a very
big, expensive project.

00:32:00.910 --> 00:32:02.920
It would have to be the
goal of civilization

00:32:02.920 --> 00:32:04.730
and maybe it should be.

00:32:04.730 --> 00:32:06.413
So, here's another map for you.

00:32:08.090 --> 00:32:12.440
We can afford to burn like 500
gigatons more of fossil fuel

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:13.360
into the atmosphere

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:18.020
before we would hit that
two degrees Celsius rise

00:32:18.020 --> 00:32:19.500
in average global temperature.

00:32:19.500 --> 00:32:21.880
That has been identified
as the maximum we can get

00:32:21.880 --> 00:32:23.950
without horrific results.

00:32:23.950 --> 00:32:27.090
500 gigatons is 500 billion tons of carbon

00:32:27.090 --> 00:32:28.560
burned into the atmosphere.

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:29.660
At the rate we're going,

00:32:29.660 --> 00:32:31.600
that's about 15 more years.

00:32:31.600 --> 00:32:33.900
But listen to this,

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:38.070
about 3,500 gigatons of fossil carbon

00:32:38.070 --> 00:32:41.140
have been identified and located

00:32:41.140 --> 00:32:46.140
by geologists and by
scientists around the world

00:32:46.310 --> 00:32:50.522
and this 3,500 gigatons of carbon,

00:32:50.522 --> 00:32:54.270
about 70% of that is
owned by nation states

00:32:54.270 --> 00:32:57.660
and about 30% by private corporations.

00:32:57.660 --> 00:33:02.660
That 3,500 gigatons of fossil carbon,

00:33:03.670 --> 00:33:07.730
3,000 gigatons needs to stay in the ground

00:33:07.730 --> 00:33:10.120
and never be burned into the atmosphere

00:33:10.120 --> 00:33:11.330
but it's already on the books

00:33:11.330 --> 00:33:13.510
as assets for the private companies

00:33:13.510 --> 00:33:17.520
and it's already in the national imaginary

00:33:17.520 --> 00:33:18.780
of the countries involved

00:33:18.780 --> 00:33:21.430
that have the most parts of this

00:33:21.430 --> 00:33:24.030
as a national security issue.

00:33:24.030 --> 00:33:24.940
That in other words,

00:33:24.940 --> 00:33:27.330
many national economies

00:33:27.330 --> 00:33:29.980
depend on sailing and
burning that fossil carbon

00:33:29.980 --> 00:33:33.130
to support everything else
that they do for their people.

00:33:33.130 --> 00:33:34.323
Now, every one of these nation states

00:33:34.323 --> 00:33:36.380
have signed the Paris agreement

00:33:36.380 --> 00:33:39.900
and has agreed to stay below two degrees.

00:33:39.900 --> 00:33:41.470
The implications of that

00:33:41.470 --> 00:33:43.890
create a logical contradiction

00:33:43.890 --> 00:33:46.380
in that many countries rely on selling

00:33:46.380 --> 00:33:48.120
and burning precisely the carbon

00:33:48.120 --> 00:33:50.860
that they've also promised
not to sell and burn.

00:33:50.860 --> 00:33:53.010
And I'm not just talking
about Saudi Arabia,

00:33:53.010 --> 00:33:54.840
although they are big amongst them

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:58.300
but also Russia, the
United States, of course,

00:33:58.300 --> 00:34:02.940
Canada, Australia, Venezuela,
Brazil, Mexico, South Africa.

00:34:05.640 --> 00:34:06.950
It goes on and on.

00:34:06.950 --> 00:34:08.930
China and India.

00:34:08.930 --> 00:34:11.630
It goes on and on that
these fossil fuel assets

00:34:11.630 --> 00:34:13.490
for these countries are on the books

00:34:13.490 --> 00:34:16.807
and the politicians are
thinking to themselves,

00:34:16.807 --> 00:34:19.337
"We'll just burn what we can get away with

00:34:19.337 --> 00:34:21.230
"before this becomes a stranded asset.'

00:34:21.230 --> 00:34:22.930
There will be a social forces

00:34:22.930 --> 00:34:27.150
trying their hardest to burn
the whole 3,500 gigatons.

00:34:27.150 --> 00:34:30.220
And because the value of those 3,000

00:34:30.220 --> 00:34:32.060
that shouldn't be burned

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:35.423
if you were to make it,
completely artificial exercise,

00:34:36.550 --> 00:34:39.100
would be approximately...

00:34:39.100 --> 00:34:40.570
I costed it once

00:34:40.570 --> 00:34:43.310
when the price of oil was a
little higher than it is now,

00:34:43.310 --> 00:34:45.900
it's something like $1,600 trillion

00:34:47.210 --> 00:34:50.300
and people are gonna want that money.

00:34:50.300 --> 00:34:52.440
So, here's an interesting map.

00:34:52.440 --> 00:34:53.490
There should be a world map

00:34:53.490 --> 00:34:55.640
that shows where all
this fossil carbon is.

00:34:55.640 --> 00:34:59.620
You could easily coal, oil, natural gas.

00:34:59.620 --> 00:35:00.930
Where are these deposit?

00:35:00.930 --> 00:35:02.320
Who owns them?

00:35:02.320 --> 00:35:04.300
How much is it worth

00:35:04.300 --> 00:35:08.800
and how do we compensate
these nations and companies?

00:35:08.800 --> 00:35:10.230
Although I think all this stuff

00:35:10.230 --> 00:35:12.100
should be nationalized immediately.

00:35:12.100 --> 00:35:13.180
There's no private company

00:35:13.180 --> 00:35:15.850
that should be in charge of fossil fuels.

00:35:15.850 --> 00:35:20.850
How do we compensate for
the loss of that money?

00:35:22.880 --> 00:35:26.800
Because each country's burn can be tracked

00:35:26.800 --> 00:35:27.990
back in time as well.

00:35:27.990 --> 00:35:29.680
It can be tracked historically

00:35:29.680 --> 00:35:31.670
right up and its present burn.

00:35:31.670 --> 00:35:33.080
Both can be tracked.

00:35:33.080 --> 00:35:34.530
And here's a rough cut.

00:35:34.530 --> 00:35:38.480
If we can burn 500 out of 3,500,

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:40.270
possibly every country

00:35:40.270 --> 00:35:44.870
should only be able to burn
one seventh of what they own

00:35:44.870 --> 00:35:47.600
and then the rest they get paid

00:35:47.600 --> 00:35:50.550
for keeping in the ground

00:35:50.550 --> 00:35:54.510
in an amortized discounted
and over time way

00:35:54.510 --> 00:35:56.840
because it would have
taken a couple of centuries

00:35:56.840 --> 00:35:58.310
to burn all that.

00:35:58.310 --> 00:35:59.630
And then in other words,

00:35:59.630 --> 00:36:03.830
there is a financial aspect
to the good Anthropocene

00:36:03.830 --> 00:36:06.060
that is crucial to think about.

00:36:06.060 --> 00:36:07.870
I wanna come back to that before I end

00:36:07.870 --> 00:36:10.690
but I see I have time for
just a couple more points.

00:36:10.690 --> 00:36:13.130
Still thinking about
the good Anthropocene,

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:17.250
we need perhaps something like
E.O. Wilson's "Half-Earth."

00:36:17.250 --> 00:36:20.610
A principle which sounded so
radical when he thought of it.

00:36:20.610 --> 00:36:22.970
That 50% of the earth should
be left to the animals,

00:36:22.970 --> 00:36:24.050
to the wild creatures

00:36:24.050 --> 00:36:27.410
and we should keep our
activities to the other 50%

00:36:27.410 --> 00:36:29.100
that's most useful to us.

00:36:29.100 --> 00:36:34.100
This sounded like a very old
and distinguished scientist

00:36:34.280 --> 00:36:35.190
going to Meredith

00:36:35.190 --> 00:36:37.870
and suggesting something
completely utopian and crazy.

00:36:37.870 --> 00:36:39.340
And then five years later,

00:36:39.340 --> 00:36:42.250
European Union and other
nation state groups

00:36:42.250 --> 00:36:45.670
are proposing 30% of the land
to be left to the animals.

00:36:45.670 --> 00:36:48.290
It's amazing how quickly that came about.

00:36:48.290 --> 00:36:53.290
And the Y2Y, Yukon-to-Yellowstone
habitat corridor,

00:36:54.030 --> 00:36:57.530
is one indication that these wild zones

00:36:57.530 --> 00:36:59.780
be connected up with each
other all over the earth.

00:36:59.780 --> 00:37:02.140
If humans congregate more in cities

00:37:02.140 --> 00:37:05.350
and then there's a carbon
draw down to re-wilding

00:37:05.350 --> 00:37:07.760
as well as a dodging of
the mass extinction event,

00:37:07.760 --> 00:37:10.913
which I claim is fundamental
to the whole project.

00:37:12.680 --> 00:37:14.550
That leads to, of course, agriculture.

00:37:14.550 --> 00:37:15.870
We gotta feed ourselves.

00:37:15.870 --> 00:37:18.290
And it's interesting to contemplate

00:37:18.290 --> 00:37:20.880
can agriculture become a carbon neutral

00:37:20.880 --> 00:37:23.890
or even a carbon negative activity?

00:37:23.890 --> 00:37:28.010
Growing of crops, ranching, aquaculture.

00:37:28.010 --> 00:37:32.180
It said that an acre of
ocean water in the shallows

00:37:32.180 --> 00:37:34.950
where kelp beds and sea grass can grow,

00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:38.810
that that acre of a shallow ocean water

00:37:38.810 --> 00:37:40.840
can hold five times more carbon

00:37:40.840 --> 00:37:42.890
than an equivalent acre of land anywhere.

00:37:44.170 --> 00:37:46.900
Indeed, carbon neutral agriculture

00:37:46.900 --> 00:37:49.170
and food production in general

00:37:49.170 --> 00:37:50.700
is gonna be on the table

00:37:50.700 --> 00:37:54.023
as the technological work
of the good Anthropocene.

00:37:55.100 --> 00:37:56.080
Very briefly,

00:37:56.080 --> 00:37:58.680
I wanna mention geoengineering

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:00.030
but only because

00:38:00.890 --> 00:38:03.370
the word has become reified,

00:38:03.370 --> 00:38:05.170
by which I mean it's turned into a thing

00:38:05.170 --> 00:38:06.757
and nobody discusses it.

00:38:06.757 --> 00:38:09.780
And what it now really
means in popular parlance,

00:38:09.780 --> 00:38:12.523
especially on the left where I live,

00:38:13.420 --> 00:38:15.130
privileged white male elite

00:38:15.130 --> 00:38:17.780
imposing a supposed silver
bullet techno solution

00:38:17.780 --> 00:38:19.270
that will save their way of life

00:38:19.270 --> 00:38:20.520
but probably backfire

00:38:20.520 --> 00:38:22.600
and make things worse for everyone else.

00:38:22.600 --> 00:38:24.010
That's geoengineering.

00:38:24.010 --> 00:38:25.330
Well, define it that way

00:38:25.330 --> 00:38:27.350
and indeed bad idea.

00:38:27.350 --> 00:38:31.100
But if you think of
large scale interventions

00:38:31.100 --> 00:38:31.950
in the biosphere

00:38:31.950 --> 00:38:34.240
hoping to reduce the damage

00:38:34.240 --> 00:38:36.190
of a global temperature rise

00:38:36.190 --> 00:38:37.460
as being a definition.

00:38:37.460 --> 00:38:41.020
Such that women's rights are
geoengineering for the good

00:38:41.020 --> 00:38:44.700
or gardening is a
geoengineering for the good.

00:38:44.700 --> 00:38:47.720
Anything we do at global scale

00:38:47.720 --> 00:38:50.560
that has impacts because
there are so many humans

00:38:50.560 --> 00:38:52.390
as small as they might be individually,

00:38:52.390 --> 00:38:53.700
if they scale up,

00:38:53.700 --> 00:38:57.040
you could think of that as geoengineering.

00:38:57.040 --> 00:39:00.560
And then if you get to the famous case

00:39:00.560 --> 00:39:02.300
of solar radiation management,

00:39:02.300 --> 00:39:06.240
can we imitate Pinatubo and
throw dust into the atmosphere,

00:39:06.240 --> 00:39:07.730
sulfur dioxide, whatever

00:39:07.730 --> 00:39:10.973
to bounce some sunlight
off before it hits earth?

00:39:12.310 --> 00:39:13.640
That would lower temperature

00:39:13.640 --> 00:39:15.740
and it might only last five years

00:39:15.740 --> 00:39:18.950
and the repercussions might
not be as significant.

00:39:18.950 --> 00:39:20.670
I mean, who can tell?

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:24.480
But if we get desperate
in the 21st century,

00:39:24.480 --> 00:39:26.350
there may be certain nation states

00:39:27.210 --> 00:39:30.040
whose people are dying from heat waves

00:39:30.040 --> 00:39:31.100
who may do this

00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:33.630
and the rest of the world
having burned the carbon,

00:39:33.630 --> 00:39:35.810
especially the developed
world in the North,

00:39:35.810 --> 00:39:37.350
will have no moral standing

00:39:37.350 --> 00:39:39.030
to objecting geoengineering

00:39:39.030 --> 00:39:40.730
just for an idea.

00:39:40.730 --> 00:39:43.900
So, it's an emergency.

00:39:43.900 --> 00:39:46.410
It would be like pulling
the emergency alarm

00:39:46.410 --> 00:39:48.840
and pulling out the fire extinguisher

00:39:48.840 --> 00:39:50.870
in a good Anthropocene.

00:39:50.870 --> 00:39:53.160
So, let me end by

00:39:53.160 --> 00:39:55.423
going back to this matter of finance.

00:39:56.470 --> 00:39:58.270
What is money?

00:39:58.270 --> 00:40:01.170
Well, it's not as easy as you might think.

00:40:01.170 --> 00:40:02.830
It's a medium of exchange.

00:40:02.830 --> 00:40:04.530
It's a store place of value.

00:40:04.530 --> 00:40:08.590
It's a demonstration of social trust.

00:40:08.590 --> 00:40:12.740
So, if you're going to do the good work

00:40:12.740 --> 00:40:16.450
of saving the biosphere and
creating a good Anthropocene,

00:40:16.450 --> 00:40:18.770
you'd need to be able
to make a living at it.

00:40:18.770 --> 00:40:21.450
And making a living is not
the same as making a profit

00:40:21.450 --> 00:40:24.060
where you extract value
and hoard it to yourself

00:40:24.060 --> 00:40:26.290
and become richer than everybody else.

00:40:26.290 --> 00:40:29.050
There's really good
work on what finance is

00:40:29.050 --> 00:40:31.290
and its relationship to the state

00:40:31.290 --> 00:40:34.470
and the nation state system and finance,

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:35.590
which is to save money.

00:40:35.590 --> 00:40:38.910
The world of finance are co-dependence

00:40:38.910 --> 00:40:41.290
and they are interdependent.

00:40:41.290 --> 00:40:42.880
People like Lazaretto say that

00:40:42.880 --> 00:40:44.520
finance controls the state.

00:40:44.520 --> 00:40:47.790
People like Joseph Vogel
and others say that

00:40:47.790 --> 00:40:49.990
the two are hand in hand

00:40:49.990 --> 00:40:53.090
but those two hands are
arm wrestling for control.

00:40:53.090 --> 00:40:55.220
I think that's a good image for it.

00:40:55.220 --> 00:40:56.210
But in any case,

00:40:56.210 --> 00:40:58.560
when finances is in control of the state,

00:40:58.560 --> 00:41:00.390
you get neo-liberal capitalism

00:41:00.390 --> 00:41:01.920
and the bad point they're at now.

00:41:01.920 --> 00:41:03.760
That doesn't work

00:41:03.760 --> 00:41:06.760
and we've seen 40 years of it misfiring

00:41:06.760 --> 00:41:10.490
despite various improvements
on other levels.

00:41:10.490 --> 00:41:11.710
So, the good Anthropocene

00:41:11.710 --> 00:41:14.450
will require state controlling finance.

00:41:14.450 --> 00:41:16.560
And if the state is

00:41:16.560 --> 00:41:19.210
of the people, by the
people, for the people

00:41:19.210 --> 00:41:20.460
in some formulation

00:41:20.460 --> 00:41:24.190
where you have good political
representation as a citizen

00:41:24.190 --> 00:41:27.810
and your governments are
essentially your organizations

00:41:27.810 --> 00:41:29.790
like a business but working for you

00:41:29.790 --> 00:41:31.470
for the good of the planet,

00:41:31.470 --> 00:41:33.450
then state controlling finance

00:41:33.450 --> 00:41:35.420
is the natural and proper order

00:41:35.420 --> 00:41:38.720
because it's people controlling
finance for the good.

00:41:38.720 --> 00:41:41.580
So, what would that kind
of finance resemble?

00:41:41.580 --> 00:41:43.250
Well, a job guarantee.

00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:45.220
If the state guarantees you a job,

00:41:45.220 --> 00:41:47.260
then unemployment as a problem goes away.

00:41:47.260 --> 00:41:48.760
But also if that job

00:41:50.226 --> 00:41:52.010
is a salary,

00:41:52.010 --> 00:41:57.010
is compensated by an adequate life

00:41:57.800 --> 00:42:00.190
so that you could get a job from the state

00:42:00.190 --> 00:42:01.570
that gave you a living,

00:42:01.570 --> 00:42:05.140
then all private industry
would have to match it.

00:42:05.140 --> 00:42:06.350
So, job guarantee,

00:42:06.350 --> 00:42:10.030
which is a fundamental
to modern monetary theory

00:42:10.030 --> 00:42:11.440
is a good idea.

00:42:11.440 --> 00:42:12.780
Progressive taxation

00:42:12.780 --> 00:42:16.880
that actually creates a ratio of

00:42:16.880 --> 00:42:20.950
if the people who are making
the most on the planet

00:42:20.950 --> 00:42:22.293
were at adequacy,

00:42:23.730 --> 00:42:26.160
that the least were at adequacy

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:29.550
and the people at the most
were making 10 times that,

00:42:29.550 --> 00:42:34.143
a one to 10 ratio in wage
parody, it's sometimes called.

00:42:35.420 --> 00:42:37.550
If you've got true adequacy,

00:42:37.550 --> 00:42:40.960
then 10 times that is stupendous luxury.

00:42:40.960 --> 00:42:42.760
But the world that we're in,

00:42:42.760 --> 00:42:47.080
it's really a ratio of one
being grossly inadequate

00:42:47.080 --> 00:42:50.920
and then the wealthiest
are like 500 times that

00:42:50.920 --> 00:42:52.860
or 1,500 times that.

00:42:52.860 --> 00:42:53.693
It's ludicrous

00:42:53.693 --> 00:42:57.430
and it could be legislated
out of existence by taxation.

00:42:57.430 --> 00:42:59.870
Then also paying off the oil companies

00:42:59.870 --> 00:43:04.870
or the nations really
that own the fossil fuels

00:43:05.430 --> 00:43:06.263
is part of it.

00:43:06.263 --> 00:43:07.390
And how can we do that?

00:43:07.390 --> 00:43:09.990
Well, money is created
by the Central Bank.

00:43:09.990 --> 00:43:14.990
So, here I'm not talking about
speculative, bubble silliness

00:43:15.140 --> 00:43:18.040
like Bitcoin, which is really just a scam.

00:43:18.040 --> 00:43:19.890
I'm talking about fiat money

00:43:19.890 --> 00:43:22.870
made by senior and by the powers that be.

00:43:22.870 --> 00:43:25.690
States backed by armies
and by their people.

00:43:25.690 --> 00:43:27.520
That's what real money is.

00:43:27.520 --> 00:43:31.760
And if real money is made to pay first

00:43:31.760 --> 00:43:33.880
for decarbonization work,

00:43:33.880 --> 00:43:36.880
for a good Anthropocene work,

00:43:36.880 --> 00:43:41.030
and it only gets paid out for that first,

00:43:41.030 --> 00:43:43.270
that's a form of quantitative easing.

00:43:43.270 --> 00:43:45.203
We know quantitative easing works.

00:43:46.737 --> 00:43:47.770
Well, sort of works

00:43:47.770 --> 00:43:48.730
but in other words,

00:43:48.730 --> 00:43:49.563
it goes out there

00:43:49.563 --> 00:43:52.350
and it doesn't completely
destabilize money as a concept.

00:43:52.350 --> 00:43:53.420
In 2008,

00:43:53.420 --> 00:43:56.290
they made up between five to $8 trillion

00:43:56.290 --> 00:43:58.210
and poured it into the system

00:43:58.210 --> 00:44:00.860
to keep the economy liquid.

00:44:00.860 --> 00:44:02.550
With the pandemic in March,

00:44:02.550 --> 00:44:04.460
they made up a couple
of trillion immediately

00:44:04.460 --> 00:44:07.060
in order to keep people from starving.

00:44:07.060 --> 00:44:09.810
So, carbon quantitative easing, CQE,

00:44:09.810 --> 00:44:11.300
is an idea that's out there.

00:44:11.300 --> 00:44:12.870
It's a great idea.

00:44:12.870 --> 00:44:14.430
You sequester carbon,

00:44:14.430 --> 00:44:15.970
you get a carbon coin.

00:44:15.970 --> 00:44:18.740
It has to be defended
by the Central Banks.

00:44:18.740 --> 00:44:21.240
It has to be made up by the
Central Banks in concept

00:44:21.240 --> 00:44:23.130
so that it isn't just a crypto coin

00:44:23.130 --> 00:44:24.320
or a speculative thing

00:44:24.320 --> 00:44:28.410
that can be manipulated by finance.

00:44:28.410 --> 00:44:30.820
It has to be solidly backed.

00:44:30.820 --> 00:44:33.280
And then you're at something solid.

00:44:33.280 --> 00:44:37.317
The civilization that pays
itself to do the right things.

00:44:37.317 --> 00:44:38.930
And we're not there yet

00:44:38.930 --> 00:44:41.270
but we have actually the mechanisms.

00:44:41.270 --> 00:44:44.200
Globalization is not bad
because we're on one planet.

00:44:44.200 --> 00:44:46.220
Bad globalization is bad

00:44:46.220 --> 00:44:48.930
like the bad Anthropocene is bad.

00:44:48.930 --> 00:44:52.150
Good globalization is in fact necessary

00:44:52.150 --> 00:44:54.810
because it can't just
happen in one country.

00:44:54.810 --> 00:44:56.650
There is no pocket utopia.

00:44:56.650 --> 00:44:57.610
It's all or nothing,

00:44:57.610 --> 00:44:59.720
which is frightening, of course.

00:44:59.720 --> 00:45:03.150
But we're on one planet and
we have no choice in that.

00:45:03.150 --> 00:45:07.740
So, I am going to end with that

00:45:07.740 --> 00:45:11.990
and turn it back over to
Priya and the rest of you

00:45:11.990 --> 00:45:16.990
and hope that we've survived our hack

00:45:17.000 --> 00:45:19.540
and maybe that serves as a model.

00:45:19.540 --> 00:45:21.460
All the good work that we do,

00:45:21.460 --> 00:45:24.110
that is going to happen
in the face of chaos

00:45:24.110 --> 00:45:26.380
and disruption and hacking

00:45:26.380 --> 00:45:29.770
and people who don't
share the same ideology

00:45:29.770 --> 00:45:32.150
and are gonna be trying to mess things up.

00:45:32.150 --> 00:45:34.250
So, what I describe

00:45:34.250 --> 00:45:38.430
is the lineaments of a
century long, wicked battle

00:45:39.500 --> 00:45:40.450
and I'll end there.

00:45:42.170 --> 00:45:44.810
- Thank you so much, Stan.

00:45:44.810 --> 00:45:45.820
That was wonderful.

00:45:45.820 --> 00:45:47.640
So, we'll open up for questions.

00:45:47.640 --> 00:45:50.860
Ty, did you want to get started with...

00:45:50.860 --> 00:45:51.823
- Yes, sure.

00:45:53.007 --> 00:45:56.250
So, a comment and perhaps
leading to a question.

00:45:56.250 --> 00:45:58.803
What would a good Anthropocene look like?

00:45:59.820 --> 00:46:02.800
The problem is more
complex, somebody noted.

00:46:02.800 --> 00:46:05.130
The sad fact is that
humans have not learned

00:46:05.130 --> 00:46:08.660
to be themselves without
being anthropocentric.

00:46:08.660 --> 00:46:11.020
So, this is what is fatal for the species,

00:46:11.020 --> 00:46:12.510
the reason being simple.

00:46:12.510 --> 00:46:15.280
No species cannot live
its life support system

00:46:16.140 --> 00:46:19.020
and the person offering this common sense

00:46:19.020 --> 00:46:20.080
will be no exception.

00:46:20.080 --> 00:46:21.730
We cannot conceive of a world

00:46:21.730 --> 00:46:23.283
in which we are not the center.

00:46:24.630 --> 00:46:25.900
Your thoughts on that.

00:46:25.900 --> 00:46:26.733
- Oh, sure.

00:46:26.733 --> 00:46:28.380
Well, I think that's right.

00:46:28.380 --> 00:46:30.500
The question is bringing up a valid point

00:46:30.500 --> 00:46:32.690
that we need to be more biocentric.

00:46:32.690 --> 00:46:35.150
We need to go back to the oldest religion,

00:46:35.150 --> 00:46:36.440
a kind of Gaia religion,

00:46:36.440 --> 00:46:38.920
where humans understand
that they are one node

00:46:38.920 --> 00:46:41.600
in a larger network of living beings

00:46:41.600 --> 00:46:44.150
and that they all are part of us.

00:46:44.150 --> 00:46:48.270
I think the news that 50%
of the DNA inside your body

00:46:48.270 --> 00:46:50.440
is not human DNA

00:46:50.440 --> 00:46:52.360
is a shocking new fact

00:46:52.360 --> 00:46:56.040
that has to change everybody
who contemplates it for awhile.

00:46:56.040 --> 00:46:58.520
That you yourself or a forest or a swamp

00:46:58.520 --> 00:47:00.420
are a biome of some kind

00:47:00.420 --> 00:47:02.620
and everything has to work inside you

00:47:02.620 --> 00:47:03.760
for you to stay healthy

00:47:03.760 --> 00:47:05.550
and then you're part of a larger system

00:47:05.550 --> 00:47:07.770
and everything has to work on that system

00:47:07.770 --> 00:47:09.100
also to stay healthy.

00:47:09.100 --> 00:47:11.320
So, at the micro and the macro,

00:47:11.320 --> 00:47:14.090
we are interpenetrated with
the rest of the biosphere

00:47:14.090 --> 00:47:16.190
and that can be a religious position

00:47:16.190 --> 00:47:17.430
and it has been in the past

00:47:17.430 --> 00:47:18.630
and it has to come back.

00:47:18.630 --> 00:47:20.740
In terms of the question
of how it would look like,

00:47:20.740 --> 00:47:22.990
let's say cities denser,

00:47:22.990 --> 00:47:24.470
lots of more open land,

00:47:24.470 --> 00:47:28.880
lots more respect for wild
animals as being our cousins,

00:47:28.880 --> 00:47:32.790
our horizontal brothers and
sisters as John Muir once said,

00:47:32.790 --> 00:47:34.870
and then go from there.

00:47:34.870 --> 00:47:36.350
It would be a work in progress

00:47:36.350 --> 00:47:37.960
and I would agree we're not there.

00:47:37.960 --> 00:47:39.300
We're too anthropocentric

00:47:39.300 --> 00:47:42.820
and it would be nice to
broaden our perspectives

00:47:42.820 --> 00:47:46.470
to actually confront the reality

00:47:46.470 --> 00:47:50.723
that we can't go it alone
as the person commented.

00:47:52.118 --> 00:47:52.951
- [Ty] Okay.

00:47:52.951 --> 00:47:54.034
- Yeah, and I think that

00:47:54.034 --> 00:47:56.120
it might also be helpful

00:47:56.120 --> 00:47:57.840
that every time we as humans

00:47:57.840 --> 00:47:58.673
have thought that

00:47:58.673 --> 00:48:00.790
we are at the center of the,
physical universe, right?

00:48:00.790 --> 00:48:02.310
We've been displayed.

00:48:02.310 --> 00:48:04.580
And hopefully now with the discovery

00:48:04.580 --> 00:48:07.740
of all these other
exoplanets and possibilities,

00:48:07.740 --> 00:48:10.430
even with just the nearby stars,

00:48:10.430 --> 00:48:12.730
harboring other planets, right?

00:48:12.730 --> 00:48:14.320
I think we need to get

00:48:14.320 --> 00:48:16.310
sort of a little bit more humble

00:48:16.310 --> 00:48:18.390
about cosmically where we are

00:48:18.390 --> 00:48:21.680
and maybe this perspective will help us

00:48:21.680 --> 00:48:24.180
become a little less anthropocentric.

00:48:24.180 --> 00:48:25.028
But I see that

00:48:25.028 --> 00:48:26.710
there is this tension

00:48:26.710 --> 00:48:31.710
between our desire to always
be focused on ourselves.

00:48:35.220 --> 00:48:38.310
But I think I really liked your point

00:48:38.310 --> 00:48:40.790
that even what we think
is the boundary of us

00:48:40.790 --> 00:48:43.320
is not really uniquely us.

00:48:43.320 --> 00:48:48.320
We have bits of DNA from
others and very complex biome.

00:48:49.850 --> 00:48:51.500
I was curious as we are waiting

00:48:51.500 --> 00:48:53.260
to use my prerogative

00:48:53.260 --> 00:48:57.930
as the organizer to ask you,

00:48:57.930 --> 00:48:59.770
So, I understand the good Anthropocene

00:48:59.770 --> 00:49:03.620
is really what we want to head toward

00:49:03.620 --> 00:49:08.240
but we see a lot of practical,
mental, psychological,

00:49:08.240 --> 00:49:10.320
aside from nation states

00:49:10.320 --> 00:49:14.020
and that level of political disagreement

00:49:14.020 --> 00:49:17.670
and ideological conflicts,

00:49:17.670 --> 00:49:20.430
to say nothing of military conflicts.

00:49:20.430 --> 00:49:24.230
At a personal, psychological,
emotional level,

00:49:24.230 --> 00:49:25.940
do you have a way of thinking

00:49:25.940 --> 00:49:29.060
about how we could all
effect that transformation

00:49:29.060 --> 00:49:30.883
sort of to build towards that?

00:49:33.700 --> 00:49:35.290
- That's a hard one

00:49:35.290 --> 00:49:39.770
because different people are
in different circumstances

00:49:39.770 --> 00:49:43.590
such that it's, I think,
dangerous or inappropriate

00:49:43.590 --> 00:49:46.000
to give advice.

00:49:46.000 --> 00:49:50.120
My situation as a suburban white man,

00:49:50.120 --> 00:49:55.120
aging house husband in a suite
eco village in California.

00:49:56.930 --> 00:50:00.270
This is an amazingly privileged position,

00:50:00.270 --> 00:50:03.010
maybe one of the most privileged
in the history of the world

00:50:03.010 --> 00:50:04.720
for a person, an individual.

00:50:04.720 --> 00:50:06.130
So, I would say

00:50:06.130 --> 00:50:08.440
take what I have to say
with a grain of salt

00:50:08.440 --> 00:50:13.440
but one can drop one's
carbon burn dramatically

00:50:13.600 --> 00:50:15.130
and we saw this with the pandemic

00:50:15.130 --> 00:50:17.770
but you could do it voluntarily as well

00:50:17.770 --> 00:50:22.230
and still have like more
fun than you were before.

00:50:22.230 --> 00:50:24.980
Less crap in your life,

00:50:24.980 --> 00:50:27.850
less worry and anxiety,

00:50:27.850 --> 00:50:30.220
walking outdoors.

00:50:30.220 --> 00:50:31.840
Just taking a simple walk

00:50:31.840 --> 00:50:35.200
is actually more satisfying and restful

00:50:35.200 --> 00:50:38.990
than almost any recreational
activity you can name,

00:50:38.990 --> 00:50:42.370
especially the ones that
involve going somewhere else

00:50:42.370 --> 00:50:45.230
and using mechanical means to get you,

00:50:45.230 --> 00:50:46.220
I don't know.

00:50:46.220 --> 00:50:49.190
I don't wanna put down
any particular activities

00:50:49.190 --> 00:50:50.400
but you know what I mean.

00:50:50.400 --> 00:50:51.460
- [Priya] Yeah.

00:50:51.460 --> 00:50:53.920
And then if you grow plants,

00:50:53.920 --> 00:50:55.900
even in pots in your apartment,

00:50:55.900 --> 00:50:59.419
immediately, the first reaction is fear.

00:50:59.419 --> 00:51:04.419
It's a stupendous technological
feat to keep ourselves fed.

00:51:05.460 --> 00:51:06.680
It's not an automatic.

00:51:06.680 --> 00:51:09.790
And so, growing plants of vegetables

00:51:09.790 --> 00:51:11.120
or even ornamentals

00:51:11.120 --> 00:51:15.550
is a huge lesson and a little
tiny bit of carbon draw down,

00:51:15.550 --> 00:51:16.900
especially with the compost heap.

00:51:16.900 --> 00:51:18.730
But also psychologically,

00:51:18.730 --> 00:51:21.040
it reorients you to reality

00:51:21.040 --> 00:51:23.110
because all of us I think are doing this

00:51:23.110 --> 00:51:25.100
and I actually I appreciate very much

00:51:25.100 --> 00:51:26.650
people coming to hear this talk

00:51:27.690 --> 00:51:30.360
given everybody's Zoom and screen fatigue

00:51:30.360 --> 00:51:31.750
and it means a lot to me

00:51:31.750 --> 00:51:33.800
because I think that it's
a gesture towards GAO

00:51:33.800 --> 00:51:36.250
and towards my novels

00:51:37.970 --> 00:51:39.070
but all of us,

00:51:39.070 --> 00:51:42.120
that needs to be integrated
with physical life

00:51:42.120 --> 00:51:45.053
in the stuff that you can
touch with your hands.

00:51:47.042 --> 00:51:49.510
And I think that that is a reorientation.

00:51:49.510 --> 00:51:51.540
It's a kind of mapping activity

00:51:51.540 --> 00:51:52.450
to go outdoors,

00:51:52.450 --> 00:51:53.570
to sit in a garden,

00:51:53.570 --> 00:51:57.610
to meet face-to-face as
socially distanced levels

00:51:57.610 --> 00:51:59.140
for the next year or so.

00:51:59.140 --> 00:51:59.973
But after that,

00:51:59.973 --> 00:52:03.300
to realize that talking to
somebody over a cup of tea

00:52:03.300 --> 00:52:07.210
is already the most pleasurable
activity that you have

00:52:07.210 --> 00:52:09.203
and it's a rather low carbon burn.

00:52:10.880 --> 00:52:14.240
- Thanks, a few more
questions have come up.

00:52:14.240 --> 00:52:16.330
Are there no examples in history

00:52:16.330 --> 00:52:17.560
from which we can learn

00:52:17.560 --> 00:52:19.763
or should we really
look for a clean break?

00:52:22.700 --> 00:52:25.563
- I think one has to be
able to learn from history.

00:52:27.770 --> 00:52:30.660
Priya mentioned my novel "Shaman."

00:52:30.660 --> 00:52:33.560
How are humans getting
by in the paleolithic?

00:52:33.560 --> 00:52:38.400
And are there things
we can learn from them?

00:52:38.400 --> 00:52:39.410
I think so,

00:52:39.410 --> 00:52:42.170
even though I often talk
about the paleolithic

00:52:42.170 --> 00:52:43.800
with good dental care.

00:52:43.800 --> 00:52:44.790
In other words,

00:52:44.790 --> 00:52:47.190
the things that we can
learn from the paleolithic

00:52:47.190 --> 00:52:49.470
is not the totality of how to live.

00:52:49.470 --> 00:52:53.290
That we are a technological
species and it's good.

00:52:53.290 --> 00:52:55.370
And so, along the way,

00:52:55.370 --> 00:52:59.160
medicine is one of the
fundamental technologies.

00:52:59.160 --> 00:53:00.890
Public health is another one.

00:53:00.890 --> 00:53:03.320
These systems that we have.

00:53:03.320 --> 00:53:04.980
I think what one wants to do

00:53:04.980 --> 00:53:07.950
is evaluate all earlier
historical societies

00:53:07.950 --> 00:53:10.890
for what was valuable in them

00:53:10.890 --> 00:53:13.640
even though they were partially enacted

00:53:13.640 --> 00:53:14.780
and pull them out

00:53:14.780 --> 00:53:17.090
and make them into a new thing.

00:53:17.090 --> 00:53:20.120
So, I don't believe in a clean break.

00:53:20.120 --> 00:53:22.280
I don't believe in a future humans

00:53:22.280 --> 00:53:23.730
that would be radically distinct

00:53:23.730 --> 00:53:26.070
from what we've been all along

00:53:26.070 --> 00:53:29.123
but there certainly is some
distinctions to be made.

00:53:32.800 --> 00:53:34.500
- Is it a Pandora's box

00:53:34.500 --> 00:53:36.640
to pay nations and organizations

00:53:36.640 --> 00:53:39.860
to not do something horrible to all of us?

00:53:39.860 --> 00:53:43.160
Is paying them out really
the best practical solution

00:53:43.160 --> 00:53:45.053
to keep oil in the ground.

00:53:46.830 --> 00:53:47.703
- Maybe not.

00:53:50.710 --> 00:53:52.830
Immediately, I think to myself,

00:53:52.830 --> 00:53:54.250
that does sound bad

00:53:54.250 --> 00:53:59.250
but I am thinking about the
way that we reward ourselves

00:53:59.330 --> 00:54:03.890
for doing useful things by way of money.

00:54:03.890 --> 00:54:08.160
And I'm thinking about what
they call climate equity,

00:54:08.160 --> 00:54:11.190
which is written into the Paris agreement

00:54:11.190 --> 00:54:16.190
that the people in the areas of the world

00:54:16.610 --> 00:54:20.510
that are gonna suffer first
from climate change effects

00:54:20.510 --> 00:54:22.220
are precisely the people

00:54:22.220 --> 00:54:25.470
who have burned the
least amount of carbon.

00:54:25.470 --> 00:54:27.010
So, the Paris agreement

00:54:27.010 --> 00:54:30.510
is a stupendously strong
document on this front

00:54:30.510 --> 00:54:34.520
because a certain cadre of
diplomats and philosophers

00:54:34.520 --> 00:54:36.440
and hardworking activists

00:54:36.440 --> 00:54:39.530
made the Paris agreement the way it is

00:54:39.530 --> 00:54:42.300
by writing these things
in with huge arguments.

00:54:42.300 --> 00:54:44.170
And what the Paris agreement says

00:54:44.170 --> 00:54:47.440
is there's a schedule one
nation's developed world

00:54:47.440 --> 00:54:49.840
that burned a lot of
carbon that are now rich.

00:54:49.840 --> 00:54:52.950
There's a developing nation scheduled to

00:54:52.950 --> 00:54:55.840
that are on the list as A and B

00:54:55.840 --> 00:55:00.030
that owe less to the payouts necessary

00:55:00.030 --> 00:55:02.510
because they've burned less carbon

00:55:02.510 --> 00:55:04.590
and have suffered more in the results.

00:55:04.590 --> 00:55:07.770
So, it wouldn't be just a case of

00:55:07.770 --> 00:55:09.310
you go to Russia and say,

00:55:09.310 --> 00:55:14.310
you've got 1,000 gigatons of carbon

00:55:14.700 --> 00:55:16.857
and therefore we are going to give you

00:55:16.857 --> 00:55:18.610
$20 trillion right now.

00:55:18.610 --> 00:55:19.443
That wouldn't work.

00:55:19.443 --> 00:55:20.980
That would crush the system.

00:55:20.980 --> 00:55:23.620
You can pressure and use leverage

00:55:23.620 --> 00:55:27.110
in the normal way of diplomacy
and of money in general,

00:55:27.110 --> 00:55:28.500
to say,

00:55:28.500 --> 00:55:30.420
look, we are going to compensate you,

00:55:30.420 --> 00:55:31.960
but it's at a discounted rate

00:55:31.960 --> 00:55:33.410
and you're gonna have
to do the right things.

00:55:33.410 --> 00:55:37.420
You're gonna have to meet
carbon sequestration scheduling

00:55:37.420 --> 00:55:38.580
in order to get the payout,

00:55:38.580 --> 00:55:41.793
but then you do get paid for
doing the virtuous thing.

00:55:43.250 --> 00:55:46.670
This is weirdly economic speak.

00:55:46.670 --> 00:55:49.600
I believe that economics is too limited

00:55:49.600 --> 00:55:51.510
of a way to talk about this

00:55:51.510 --> 00:55:53.960
but we organize global society

00:55:53.960 --> 00:55:56.650
by way of economic considerations.

00:55:56.650 --> 00:55:58.790
And one part of economics

00:55:58.790 --> 00:56:01.670
is a real and genuine social science

00:56:01.670 --> 00:56:03.930
trying to find the best way forward.

00:56:03.930 --> 00:56:05.700
The other part is just an excuse

00:56:05.700 --> 00:56:08.180
for a power play of
the rich over the poor.

00:56:08.180 --> 00:56:10.480
You gotta destrand economics

00:56:10.480 --> 00:56:13.130
and get to the part that's useful to us.

00:56:13.130 --> 00:56:16.340
- But isn't some of
this, what you suggest,

00:56:16.340 --> 00:56:20.563
still within the framework
of transactional capitalism?

00:56:21.910 --> 00:56:24.430
It's still very much that rubric, right?

00:56:24.430 --> 00:56:28.750
So, I guess parts of
that we have to live with

00:56:28.750 --> 00:56:31.130
because that's what we're built on.

00:56:31.130 --> 00:56:35.663
Can't fundamentally shake
that apart in one go.

00:56:36.590 --> 00:56:37.740
- Well, not in one go.

00:56:37.740 --> 00:56:41.840
I like a stepwise march
to a true post-capitalism,

00:56:41.840 --> 00:56:43.400
but given where we are now,

00:56:43.400 --> 00:56:47.023
I would say anti-austerity, Keynesianism,

00:56:48.540 --> 00:56:50.280
social democracies,

00:56:50.280 --> 00:56:53.320
democratic socialism, post-capitalism

00:56:53.320 --> 00:56:54.910
as a stepwise march

00:56:54.910 --> 00:56:57.550
from one legal stage to the next

00:56:57.550 --> 00:56:59.143
so that it's more orderly,

00:57:00.300 --> 00:57:01.860
hopefully less violent

00:57:01.860 --> 00:57:04.120
and more effective.

00:57:04.120 --> 00:57:05.570
So, I, myself,

00:57:05.570 --> 00:57:08.740
I'm a full-on post-capitalist.

00:57:08.740 --> 00:57:11.920
I think capitalism is just
the name for a power system,

00:57:11.920 --> 00:57:12.980
but we're in it.

00:57:12.980 --> 00:57:14.800
And certainly money will still exist.

00:57:14.800 --> 00:57:17.410
Certainly capital will still exist,

00:57:17.410 --> 00:57:19.610
but what we pay ourselves to do

00:57:19.610 --> 00:57:21.660
and how we own capital,

00:57:21.660 --> 00:57:23.103
these are the big questions.

00:57:29.520 --> 00:57:30.370
- As you know,

00:57:30.370 --> 00:57:32.920
one American has the carbon footprint

00:57:32.920 --> 00:57:35.570
equivalent to 20 Bangladeshi.

00:57:35.570 --> 00:57:38.420
U.S. makes up 5% of the world's population

00:57:38.420 --> 00:57:40.950
but using 25% of the world's energy.

00:57:40.950 --> 00:57:43.540
What do you think of
Naomi Klein's perspective

00:57:43.540 --> 00:57:45.840
on Anthropocene discourse

00:57:45.840 --> 00:57:48.150
that the U.S. has a greater responsibility

00:57:48.150 --> 00:57:50.120
in reducing the carbon footprint

00:57:50.120 --> 00:57:51.853
than any other country on earth?

00:57:54.050 --> 00:57:56.170
- I am in solidarity with Naomi Klein,

00:57:56.170 --> 00:57:58.043
and I think she's right about that.

00:57:58.950 --> 00:58:01.310
And then historically and accurately,

00:58:01.310 --> 00:58:03.100
you have to immediately pull in

00:58:03.100 --> 00:58:08.100
also China and the European
Union and soon enough India,

00:58:08.920 --> 00:58:11.470
but these are at differential
rates historically.

00:58:11.470 --> 00:58:12.730
So, climate equity,

00:58:12.730 --> 00:58:13.620
climate justice

00:58:13.620 --> 00:58:17.280
would require that the
United States leading the way

00:58:17.280 --> 00:58:19.800
would make a huge difference.

00:58:19.800 --> 00:58:22.100
You see small countries operating

00:58:22.100 --> 00:58:26.396
with a stupendous care for their land

00:58:26.396 --> 00:58:28.680
and with a very low carbon burn.

00:58:28.680 --> 00:58:33.230
And this includes developed
countries like Switzerland

00:58:33.230 --> 00:58:36.300
and developing countries like Cuba,

00:58:36.300 --> 00:58:40.300
which often rates highest on human welfare

00:58:40.300 --> 00:58:42.853
versus carbon burn rubrics.

00:58:43.750 --> 00:58:47.610
So, Naomi Klein has done stupendous work

00:58:47.610 --> 00:58:50.610
for clarifying these issues
to a general audience.

00:58:50.610 --> 00:58:53.650
And I hope that her next
book is about the global,

00:58:53.650 --> 00:58:55.050
about international finance

00:58:55.050 --> 00:58:59.160
because although her
focus on local resistance

00:58:59.160 --> 00:59:01.380
and on regional moves,

00:59:01.380 --> 00:59:03.100
these are all necessary.

00:59:03.100 --> 00:59:04.910
They're necessary but
they're not sufficient

00:59:04.910 --> 00:59:06.080
because we're in this

00:59:06.080 --> 00:59:08.210
international neo-liberal capital system

00:59:08.210 --> 00:59:09.900
that will simply extract value

00:59:09.900 --> 00:59:13.350
from the top of even good local work.

00:59:13.350 --> 00:59:15.270
So, I think she knows this already

00:59:15.270 --> 00:59:20.030
and I did an event with her
very recently at Rutgers.

00:59:20.030 --> 00:59:21.440
And all I can is that

00:59:21.440 --> 00:59:26.440
it would be great if her work
is as widespread as possible.

00:59:29.270 --> 00:59:32.380
- Great, so Chao Carlos,

00:59:32.380 --> 00:59:34.180
so we have a question from Carlo Rovelli.

00:59:34.180 --> 00:59:37.870
He says everything that
you say is very reasonable,

00:59:37.870 --> 00:59:41.730
and of course he likes and
falls in the same camp,

00:59:41.730 --> 00:59:42.900
but he says

00:59:42.900 --> 00:59:44.650
what I see missing is the political side.

00:59:44.650 --> 00:59:45.510
In history,

00:59:45.510 --> 00:59:47.010
rarely people have collaborated

00:59:47.010 --> 00:59:49.580
just out of rational
thinking and common good.

00:59:49.580 --> 00:59:51.420
Competition has always dominated

00:59:51.420 --> 00:59:53.410
unless imposed by a power.

00:59:53.410 --> 00:59:56.120
How do we move politically
towards collaboration?

00:59:56.120 --> 00:59:59.777
And we had a very similar
question along the same lines

00:59:59.777 --> 01:00:02.110
from John Peters who said,

01:00:02.110 --> 01:00:04.390
recently our country voted for a candidate

01:00:04.390 --> 01:00:06.840
who openly opposed this kind of thinking.

01:00:06.840 --> 01:00:09.860
So, how do you handle political roadblocks

01:00:09.860 --> 01:00:11.473
to the good Anthropocene?

01:00:12.580 --> 01:00:15.100
- Well, for me, there are two levels here.

01:00:15.100 --> 01:00:16.980
One is the discursive battle.

01:00:16.980 --> 01:00:17.930
In other words,

01:00:17.930 --> 01:00:18.900
it's like this.

01:00:18.900 --> 01:00:20.020
It's talking about it.

01:00:20.020 --> 01:00:21.560
It's convincing enough people,

01:00:21.560 --> 01:00:23.190
persuading people

01:00:23.190 --> 01:00:26.010
that your ideological explanation

01:00:26.010 --> 01:00:27.640
for what's going on in the world

01:00:27.640 --> 01:00:29.970
is correct enough to act upon.

01:00:29.970 --> 01:00:32.930
So, the discursive battle
is very, very important.

01:00:32.930 --> 01:00:34.478
And then I have to say,

01:00:34.478 --> 01:00:39.310
you're only ever gonna get
a working majority at best.

01:00:39.310 --> 01:00:43.930
So, you have to give up on
the notion of a consensus

01:00:43.930 --> 01:00:45.300
or have a huge majority.

01:00:45.300 --> 01:00:50.300
Despite the obvious utility
of saving the biosphere,

01:00:51.740 --> 01:00:53.390
a lot of people don't see it that way.

01:00:53.390 --> 01:00:58.390
And with the most vigorous,
discursive, persuasive battle,

01:00:58.520 --> 01:00:59.820
they're still not gonna see it that way

01:00:59.820 --> 01:01:01.320
because people love their views

01:01:01.320 --> 01:01:03.330
and none of us likes to change our views.

01:01:03.330 --> 01:01:07.770
So, a working majority is 51%
like in the general election,

01:01:07.770 --> 01:01:09.430
but then there's the electoral college.

01:01:09.430 --> 01:01:12.210
A working majority is sometimes up to 60%

01:01:12.210 --> 01:01:13.620
before you can actually work,

01:01:13.620 --> 01:01:17.110
like in certain votes in
the U.S. Senate at 60%.

01:01:17.110 --> 01:01:20.693
A high bar in the current divisive world,

01:01:21.830 --> 01:01:25.530
I have to say fossil fuel
extractive industries

01:01:25.530 --> 01:01:28.130
are pouring immense amounts of money

01:01:28.130 --> 01:01:30.160
into disinformation campaigns

01:01:30.160 --> 01:01:32.760
and into buying the votes of legislators

01:01:32.760 --> 01:01:34.450
who are elected on one platform

01:01:34.450 --> 01:01:35.880
and then vote on another

01:01:35.880 --> 01:01:38.620
and support the continued carbon burn.

01:01:38.620 --> 01:01:40.858
Well, this one has to become unacceptable

01:01:40.858 --> 01:01:43.010
and that's partly the discursive battle

01:01:43.010 --> 01:01:44.480
and partly political organizing

01:01:44.480 --> 01:01:47.950
and partly fighting for different laws.

01:01:47.950 --> 01:01:50.874
So, the working majority
can change the laws

01:01:50.874 --> 01:01:55.198
and then the laws can change
the structure of feeling

01:01:55.198 --> 01:01:59.050
and the economic system together at once.

01:01:59.050 --> 01:02:01.660
So, I don't have any good answers for this

01:02:01.660 --> 01:02:02.730
because no one does.

01:02:02.730 --> 01:02:04.510
I mean, it's politics, right?

01:02:04.510 --> 01:02:07.530
It's ugly, it's irrational.

01:02:07.530 --> 01:02:10.170
It has to do with a very tribal sensations

01:02:10.170 --> 01:02:11.870
of us versus them.

01:02:11.870 --> 01:02:15.010
And it's nice to try to
pull away from that and say,

01:02:15.010 --> 01:02:17.020
but wait, everybody's on the same planet

01:02:17.020 --> 01:02:19.240
and everybody loves their children.

01:02:19.240 --> 01:02:21.480
And there you get back
to the discursive battle.

01:02:21.480 --> 01:02:22.740
Well, that being the case,

01:02:22.740 --> 01:02:26.790
can you persuade people
that decarbonization

01:02:26.790 --> 01:02:30.470
and a new way of living with the biosphere

01:02:30.470 --> 01:02:32.320
is just in everybody's best interest.

01:02:34.690 --> 01:02:36.440
I think you can tell from what I'm saying,

01:02:36.440 --> 01:02:38.754
that this is a way of saying that

01:02:38.754 --> 01:02:43.754
there's nothing but continued struggle.

01:02:47.020 --> 01:02:50.400
- I have centric communities
are all small communities.

01:02:50.400 --> 01:02:53.670
Maybe one solution is to
have small local communities.

01:02:53.670 --> 01:02:56.000
And I wonder whether COVID
has given us glimmers

01:02:56.000 --> 01:02:58.190
of what that might look and feel like,

01:02:58.190 --> 01:03:00.013
one participant suggests.

01:03:01.920 --> 01:03:04.293
- Well, I wonder about that.

01:03:05.170 --> 01:03:10.170
And I think it's a human fact
that we like our community.

01:03:12.350 --> 01:03:14.540
My teacher, Gary Snyder once talked about

01:03:14.540 --> 01:03:16.570
community versus network.

01:03:16.570 --> 01:03:18.550
So, your community or
the people that you see

01:03:18.550 --> 01:03:20.780
and talk to and interact with.

01:03:20.780 --> 01:03:22.810
And of course, in, in the COVID regime,

01:03:22.810 --> 01:03:23.690
this is very weird.

01:03:23.690 --> 01:03:25.560
Our communities have been damaged.

01:03:25.560 --> 01:03:27.640
Your network is everybody
that you're connected with

01:03:27.640 --> 01:03:31.040
all over the world, by
this medium and others.

01:03:31.040 --> 01:03:36.040
So, at the community level,
you can organize your town.

01:03:36.370 --> 01:03:39.290
You can actually make an impact
at the level of your town

01:03:39.290 --> 01:03:40.800
to do better around climate.

01:03:40.800 --> 01:03:43.040
American towns are trying their best

01:03:43.040 --> 01:03:46.470
to confront this crisis by
cutting their carbon burn

01:03:46.470 --> 01:03:48.010
at the town level.

01:03:48.010 --> 01:03:50.620
That is absolutely great.

01:03:50.620 --> 01:03:52.623
And something that we need to focus on.

01:03:52.623 --> 01:03:53.570
And not only that,

01:03:53.570 --> 01:03:54.700
but when you focus on that,

01:03:54.700 --> 01:03:56.240
you can see results of your work.

01:03:56.240 --> 01:03:58.980
You can talk to people
it's social activity

01:03:58.980 --> 01:04:00.070
with some results.

01:04:00.070 --> 01:04:02.170
The international situation is so...

01:04:02.170 --> 01:04:03.880
I mean, you're 18 billion

01:04:03.880 --> 01:04:05.760
of the power structure of the planet,

01:04:05.760 --> 01:04:07.290
but a lot of people have more money

01:04:07.290 --> 01:04:09.410
and therefore more power than you do.

01:04:09.410 --> 01:04:12.860
That's so abstract that it ends up

01:04:12.860 --> 01:04:14.920
at the level of discourse.

01:04:14.920 --> 01:04:17.910
So, I would say essentially
that you needed it all.

01:04:17.910 --> 01:04:20.100
And that I ended up
talking about the global

01:04:20.100 --> 01:04:21.300
and partly because,

01:04:21.300 --> 01:04:23.060
well, Naomi Klein and others are great

01:04:23.060 --> 01:04:24.320
at talking about the regional,

01:04:24.320 --> 01:04:27.493
and then everybody is involved
with their own local efforts.

01:04:28.350 --> 01:04:32.480
And, I would say that these are all great,

01:04:32.480 --> 01:04:35.950
but that they aren't sufficient
to complete the deal,

01:04:35.950 --> 01:04:38.760
which is to change the rules

01:04:38.760 --> 01:04:41.100
of international capitalism itself

01:04:41.100 --> 01:04:42.360
at the level of treaties,

01:04:42.360 --> 01:04:45.120
at the level of central
banks at the level of money.

01:04:45.120 --> 01:04:46.780
I bring that into the discourse,

01:04:46.780 --> 01:04:48.630
not because I think it's the silver bullet

01:04:48.630 --> 01:04:50.270
or the total solution,

01:04:50.270 --> 01:04:52.610
but because I think it is a necessary part

01:04:52.610 --> 01:04:53.970
of all the rest of the efforts.

01:04:53.970 --> 01:04:56.650
It has to be integrated from...

01:04:56.650 --> 01:04:58.440
And it isn't even good
to say bottom to top.

01:04:58.440 --> 01:05:02.530
It has to be integrated
from a local to planetary,

01:05:02.530 --> 01:05:03.630
let's put it that way.

01:05:04.790 --> 01:05:09.500
- So Tim asks, what can be
the role for higher education

01:05:09.500 --> 01:05:11.600
in the face of the
kinds of transformations

01:05:11.600 --> 01:05:13.083
that you were calling for?

01:05:14.490 --> 01:05:16.272
- Well, I am interested.

01:05:16.272 --> 01:05:17.720
I'm an ex academic,

01:05:17.720 --> 01:05:20.830
and I'm always involved with
universities like Yale here.

01:05:20.830 --> 01:05:21.760
I do a lot.

01:05:21.760 --> 01:05:23.680
And I'm thinking, talking to young people,

01:05:23.680 --> 01:05:26.960
they are actually more
flexible in their ideologies

01:05:26.960 --> 01:05:29.340
and they're learning to
shape their ideologies

01:05:29.340 --> 01:05:32.740
and where they're gonna serve
their efforts in a career.

01:05:32.740 --> 01:05:35.900
I think there ought to be a freshmen class

01:05:35.900 --> 01:05:37.580
that I see very clearly in my mind.

01:05:37.580 --> 01:05:40.950
I mean, I'm talking about the
syllabus and the readings,

01:05:40.950 --> 01:05:44.810
and that would in their
first year of college,

01:05:44.810 --> 01:05:47.670
give them a cognitive mapping.

01:05:47.670 --> 01:05:50.610
Like I just tried here, an
orientation to the world.

01:05:50.610 --> 01:05:52.680
This is the world situation.

01:05:52.680 --> 01:05:54.430
There's a science fiction element to that,

01:05:54.430 --> 01:05:55.720
that I've been incorporating.

01:05:55.720 --> 01:05:57.470
I talked to students who are,

01:05:57.470 --> 01:05:59.350
let's say they're 20 years old.

01:05:59.350 --> 01:06:02.020
Let's say I'm very close to 70 years old,

01:06:02.020 --> 01:06:03.940
a half century gap.

01:06:03.940 --> 01:06:06.130
I tell them when you're my age,

01:06:06.130 --> 01:06:08.440
it will be the year 2070.

01:06:08.440 --> 01:06:11.230
You will be in a
completely different world

01:06:11.230 --> 01:06:14.550
by necessity because of the
way things are going now

01:06:14.550 --> 01:06:16.010
cannot be sustained.

01:06:16.010 --> 01:06:18.350
It will change in their lifetimes.

01:06:18.350 --> 01:06:21.430
So, higher education ought to be seized,

01:06:21.430 --> 01:06:24.420
the professorial class
and the administrations.

01:06:24.420 --> 01:06:26.320
And these are actually groups

01:06:26.320 --> 01:06:27.530
that are at odds with each other.

01:06:27.530 --> 01:06:30.240
Often the academic sentence, let's say,

01:06:30.240 --> 01:06:32.200
since they're still in
control of pedagogy,

01:06:32.200 --> 01:06:33.580
to a certain extent,

01:06:33.580 --> 01:06:36.340
they ought to be looking at
this situation and saying,

01:06:36.340 --> 01:06:38.750
we need to orient these young people,

01:06:38.750 --> 01:06:40.330
and send them out into the world

01:06:40.330 --> 01:06:45.330
with a well-organized cognitive map

01:06:45.400 --> 01:06:48.050
for figuring out what they're
gonna do with their lives

01:06:48.050 --> 01:06:49.110
without getting scared,

01:06:49.110 --> 01:06:50.150
without freaking out,

01:06:50.150 --> 01:06:55.140
without sending them off into
various kinds of doomerism.

01:06:55.140 --> 01:06:56.040
Like, okay,

01:06:56.040 --> 01:06:58.320
the world is so doomed that
I'm just gonna go party.

01:06:58.320 --> 01:07:00.140
Well, most young college students

01:07:00.140 --> 01:07:03.560
are not going to college in
order to take that attitude.

01:07:03.560 --> 01:07:07.170
So, they're well set for an education

01:07:07.170 --> 01:07:08.780
that would teach them these things

01:07:08.780 --> 01:07:11.413
and get them oriented to
the rest of their lives.

01:07:13.130 --> 01:07:16.460
- So then we have a question
that touches a little bit

01:07:16.460 --> 01:07:20.020
on the personnel, in which full-on us,

01:07:20.020 --> 01:07:21.040
they're interested in,

01:07:21.040 --> 01:07:24.590
whether the experience of
having worked as a house husband

01:07:24.590 --> 01:07:26.680
has informed your perspective.

01:07:26.680 --> 01:07:29.870
Do you think a transfer
of more power to women

01:07:29.870 --> 01:07:32.090
may radically change our ability

01:07:32.090 --> 01:07:33.853
towards the good Anthropocene?

01:07:34.810 --> 01:07:35.643
- Oh, yes.

01:07:35.643 --> 01:07:36.580
Definitely so.

01:07:36.580 --> 01:07:39.410
And I want to thank
the questioner for that

01:07:39.410 --> 01:07:43.310
because it's important when women have

01:07:43.310 --> 01:07:46.270
their full set of legal rights,

01:07:46.270 --> 01:07:48.410
and I'm not talking about
reproductive rights,

01:07:48.410 --> 01:07:51.880
I'm talking about property,
education and general,

01:07:51.880 --> 01:07:54.040
the rights that they are guaranteed

01:07:54.040 --> 01:07:56.720
by the UN declaration of human rights.

01:07:56.720 --> 01:07:59.360
The reproduction rate goes down

01:07:59.360 --> 01:08:01.510
to below the replacement rate.

01:08:01.510 --> 01:08:06.510
So, replacement rate is
2.1 or 2.2 kids per woman

01:08:07.830 --> 01:08:11.100
in developed countries where
women have their rights

01:08:11.100 --> 01:08:13.343
and are busy with their lives.

01:08:14.650 --> 01:08:18.970
It goes down to about
1.5 to 1.8 per woman.

01:08:18.970 --> 01:08:21.000
And the population begins to drop

01:08:21.000 --> 01:08:23.600
and the pressure on the
biosphere goes away.

01:08:23.600 --> 01:08:27.280
And then also, this
brings up all of the work

01:08:27.280 --> 01:08:31.420
that mostly women do that
keeps alive the world

01:08:31.420 --> 01:08:36.300
that then capitalism can be a
parasite on and make profit.

01:08:36.300 --> 01:08:39.170
There is no profit without
social reproduction,

01:08:39.170 --> 01:08:43.120
and that's a word for
uncompensated labor of care.

01:08:43.120 --> 01:08:44.530
So, taking care of the little kids,

01:08:44.530 --> 01:08:47.320
educating the littlest kids and

01:08:47.320 --> 01:08:50.140
nursing and taking care of the elderly,

01:08:50.140 --> 01:08:53.190
a lot of this is outside of the economy.

01:08:53.190 --> 01:08:55.290
And if it were included in the economy,

01:08:55.290 --> 01:08:56.530
profits would be wiped out.

01:08:56.530 --> 01:08:59.560
So, that's on one of the ways
in which profit is a scam,

01:08:59.560 --> 01:09:02.800
but also you don't want to
economize care and love.

01:09:02.800 --> 01:09:03.787
So, you can't just say,

01:09:03.787 --> 01:09:05.217
"Okay, I took care of my grandmother,

01:09:05.217 --> 01:09:07.150
"pay me $20 for that hour."

01:09:07.150 --> 01:09:10.810
No, what you would need
is a political economy

01:09:10.810 --> 01:09:15.130
that created the space
that people could have

01:09:15.130 --> 01:09:17.080
an adequate living

01:09:17.080 --> 01:09:20.120
and still do the human work
of social reproduction.

01:09:20.120 --> 01:09:23.100
Well, most of the world

01:09:23.100 --> 01:09:24.920
women's simply have taken on the burden

01:09:24.920 --> 01:09:26.520
of doing both of these things at once.

01:09:26.520 --> 01:09:29.840
And this is why there's so
much pressure on women's lives,

01:09:29.840 --> 01:09:31.840
is they've got a double job.

01:09:31.840 --> 01:09:35.840
One, to make money in the
economy and do their work.

01:09:35.840 --> 01:09:39.990
B, to take care of their
families, both older and younger,

01:09:39.990 --> 01:09:41.880
and maybe even partners.

01:09:41.880 --> 01:09:44.990
And this is simply like 20 hours a day.

01:09:44.990 --> 01:09:49.990
So yeah, it is super important

01:09:51.570 --> 01:09:53.580
to support the women in the world,

01:09:53.580 --> 01:09:54.480
to be a feminist.

01:09:54.480 --> 01:09:56.457
Men need to say, "Oh yeah, I'm a feminist,

01:09:56.457 --> 01:09:58.747
"but only in a follower's kind of a way,

01:09:58.747 --> 01:10:00.630
"not a leaders kind of way."

01:10:00.630 --> 01:10:02.490
And also see that the laws have changed

01:10:02.490 --> 01:10:03.900
and that the rest of the world,

01:10:03.900 --> 01:10:06.070
I mean, if the United States is the leader

01:10:06.070 --> 01:10:06.990
and it's not quite true,

01:10:06.990 --> 01:10:10.390
you're scanning Navy as
a leader in these issues.

01:10:10.390 --> 01:10:11.890
And there are many places in the world

01:10:11.890 --> 01:10:14.280
beginning to emphasize women's rights.

01:10:14.280 --> 01:10:18.400
Then they have to work hard
to pull the rest of the world

01:10:18.400 --> 01:10:19.290
along in that.

01:10:19.290 --> 01:10:22.410
And that will really help biosphere health

01:10:22.410 --> 01:10:26.040
and human welfare, yes.

01:10:26.040 --> 01:10:26.873
And by the way, thank you for that.

01:10:26.873 --> 01:10:31.074
Because that really ought
to be an element of my talk

01:10:31.074 --> 01:10:32.873
and it often is.

01:10:33.860 --> 01:10:35.650
- So, in the red moon,

01:10:35.650 --> 01:10:38.860
you mentioned the concept
of blockchain democracy.

01:10:38.860 --> 01:10:40.050
Do you want to discuss that

01:10:40.050 --> 01:10:42.740
in the context of the future Anthropocene?

01:10:42.740 --> 01:10:45.133
Does it have a room for that?

01:10:46.090 --> 01:10:47.790
- Well, I think so.

01:10:47.790 --> 01:10:49.821
Let me confess that blockchain

01:10:49.821 --> 01:10:52.282
is difficult to understand technology

01:10:52.282 --> 01:10:55.370
and it often gets confused with Bitcoin,

01:10:55.370 --> 01:10:56.573
which I quite hate,

01:10:57.740 --> 01:11:00.663
but blockchain as a system of encryption

01:11:00.663 --> 01:11:05.530
that is distributed and
solidified our information

01:11:05.530 --> 01:11:07.370
about things so that there,

01:11:07.370 --> 01:11:09.280
it creates a more transparent world,

01:11:09.280 --> 01:11:13.700
as well as a more secure
world of digital information.

01:11:13.700 --> 01:11:16.420
I can imagine money being blockchain,

01:11:16.420 --> 01:11:18.310
such that you can't have tax havens

01:11:18.310 --> 01:11:20.480
or hidden criminal money.

01:11:20.480 --> 01:11:22.680
I can imagine democracy
being in blockchain,

01:11:24.400 --> 01:11:27.790
and this is true at town
levels in the United States.

01:11:27.790 --> 01:11:29.200
It's open government.

01:11:29.200 --> 01:11:30.830
It's illegal to meet in private.

01:11:30.830 --> 01:11:34.310
And then blockchain would
kind of digitalize that.

01:11:34.310 --> 01:11:39.170
I'm a little confused as to
the utility of blockchain.

01:11:39.170 --> 01:11:40.530
Sometimes people tell me,

01:11:40.530 --> 01:11:43.390
well, it's just a buzzword for codes

01:11:43.390 --> 01:11:45.290
that are actually simpler

01:11:45.290 --> 01:11:47.270
that are completely available to us.

01:11:47.270 --> 01:11:49.190
So, why even use that word?

01:11:49.190 --> 01:11:52.990
And you don't want the
repetitiousness of Bitcoin's creation

01:11:52.990 --> 01:11:56.810
of value by way of running
computers for zillions of hours

01:11:56.810 --> 01:11:58.300
to no purpose.

01:11:58.300 --> 01:12:01.370
It's something like 2%
of the world's energy

01:12:01.370 --> 01:12:03.960
is being burned in order to make Bitcoin.

01:12:03.960 --> 01:12:07.390
Well, this is just
carbon burn foolishness,

01:12:07.390 --> 01:12:10.240
is something like the
economy of a Czech Republic

01:12:10.240 --> 01:12:14.120
or even Israel being burned
for nothing except for a scam.

01:12:14.120 --> 01:12:16.870
So, I'm not so sure about blockchain.

01:12:16.870 --> 01:12:19.520
I would need the digital
experts to weigh in on that.

01:12:20.790 --> 01:12:24.820
- So there's a comment from Yanni Joseph.

01:12:24.820 --> 01:12:28.453
How about the need to
drastically cut military budgets?

01:12:29.890 --> 01:12:32.970
- Well, that would be
fantastic, especially.

01:12:32.970 --> 01:12:35.440
Yeah, the U.S military budget is bigger

01:12:35.440 --> 01:12:38.140
than all the rest of the
military budgets combined.

01:12:38.140 --> 01:12:41.670
And the U.S has 800 military
bases placed around the world

01:12:41.670 --> 01:12:46.670
and is a functioning low
key to functioning empire.

01:12:46.740 --> 01:12:48.503
And that doesn't work.

01:12:51.410 --> 01:12:53.520
What I can say about the military is

01:12:54.730 --> 01:12:56.860
I'm looking at the Swiss
military in particular,

01:12:56.860 --> 01:13:00.690
but also all those militaries
that end up being kind of

01:13:00.690 --> 01:13:03.490
an emergency rescue services.

01:13:03.490 --> 01:13:07.730
And in the United States, the U.S Navy,

01:13:07.730 --> 01:13:09.620
I mean the U.S military burns

01:13:09.620 --> 01:13:12.240
a stupendous amount of carbon uselessly,

01:13:12.240 --> 01:13:14.150
but they're also aware that

01:13:14.150 --> 01:13:18.870
decarbonization is a
national security issue.

01:13:18.870 --> 01:13:23.410
And the Navy has run a nuclear
fleet without accidents

01:13:23.410 --> 01:13:25.130
because they don't try
to make a profit from it.

01:13:25.130 --> 01:13:26.750
And safety comes first.

01:13:26.750 --> 01:13:29.480
They also have been involved
in rescue operations,

01:13:29.480 --> 01:13:31.770
and it's also true that in modern warfare,

01:13:31.770 --> 01:13:33.820
navies are all sunk to the bottom

01:13:33.820 --> 01:13:35.690
in the first week of a war.

01:13:35.690 --> 01:13:38.220
And so, they need to be
thinking about rescue

01:13:38.220 --> 01:13:40.720
and non-military confrontation.

01:13:40.720 --> 01:13:43.160
And in short,

01:13:43.160 --> 01:13:46.730
if the world got together
on this carbon coin,

01:13:46.730 --> 01:13:48.820
if civilization was devoted

01:13:48.820 --> 01:13:52.140
to trying to avoid the
mass extinction event,

01:13:52.140 --> 01:13:54.120
then the militaries would end up being

01:13:54.120 --> 01:13:56.120
like the CCC ONE hopes,

01:13:56.120 --> 01:13:59.230
a place of service and not have conflict.

01:13:59.230 --> 01:14:01.360
And then their budgets of
course could go way down

01:14:01.360 --> 01:14:06.360
on the extremely expensive armaments race.

01:14:07.878 --> 01:14:12.110
This could be brought to
an end by mutual agreement.

01:14:12.110 --> 01:14:13.860
And should be part of the big deal.

01:14:15.100 --> 01:14:17.750
- So, let's close with the final question,

01:14:17.750 --> 01:14:18.960
which is about a word.

01:14:18.960 --> 01:14:21.150
So, I thought this might be a nice way.

01:14:21.150 --> 01:14:23.960
So a vibrant biosphere
and adequacy for all,

01:14:23.960 --> 01:14:27.020
along with the global security
which would go with that,

01:14:27.020 --> 01:14:29.740
would be a very charismatic goal.

01:14:29.740 --> 01:14:34.120
Could we have come up with a
better term than just adequacy?

01:14:34.120 --> 01:14:36.037
Would this (mumbles) be the border

01:14:36.037 --> 01:14:37.702
of a powerful political movement?

01:14:37.702 --> 01:14:39.365
(laughs)

01:14:39.365 --> 01:14:42.690
- Yeah, adequate is not
is not exactly exciting.

01:14:42.690 --> 01:14:47.094
Well, okay, here's something
that I want you to contemplate.

01:14:47.094 --> 01:14:50.380
It's an old English saying
in Bartletts quotations,

01:14:50.380 --> 01:14:52.560
it comes on like page seven or something,

01:14:52.560 --> 01:14:53.880
and it's anonymous.

01:14:53.880 --> 01:14:56.650
Enough is as good as a feast.

01:14:56.650 --> 01:15:00.646
Now, it was hungry people
that brought up that,

01:15:00.646 --> 01:15:02.480
that made up that proverb

01:15:02.480 --> 01:15:03.930
and they knew what they were talking about

01:15:03.930 --> 01:15:07.310
because there were
feasts from time to time

01:15:07.310 --> 01:15:08.620
when the harvests were good,

01:15:08.620 --> 01:15:11.070
but enough is as good as a feast.

01:15:11.070 --> 01:15:14.860
The more you think about
that, the deeper it goes.

01:15:14.860 --> 01:15:15.930
So, okay.

01:15:15.930 --> 01:15:18.603
Enough is sort of a word like adequate,

01:15:19.660 --> 01:15:21.070
but you need the whole phrase,

01:15:21.070 --> 01:15:22.790
enough is as good as a feast

01:15:22.790 --> 01:15:25.130
and indeed a feast being bad for you.

01:15:25.130 --> 01:15:26.830
You could change the old proverb and say

01:15:26.830 --> 01:15:29.090
enough is better than a feast.

01:15:29.090 --> 01:15:34.090
So, the word very often has been utopia.

01:15:34.130 --> 01:15:35.920
The word has been utopia.

01:15:35.920 --> 01:15:37.810
Now, the same way

01:15:37.810 --> 01:15:40.270
ideology is what the
other person believes,

01:15:40.270 --> 01:15:42.970
utopia is the good place
that we never get to,

01:15:42.970 --> 01:15:44.410
that is impossible.

01:15:44.410 --> 01:15:46.193
So, definitions matter.

01:15:47.080 --> 01:15:50.950
But I have been working
in the utopian tradition

01:15:50.950 --> 01:15:54.336
my whole career with the idea that

01:15:54.336 --> 01:15:56.650
a better world is possible,

01:15:56.650 --> 01:15:59.870
and you need to describe it
first in order to get to it.

01:15:59.870 --> 01:16:04.870
So, I'm constantly
defending the term utopian,

01:16:04.900 --> 01:16:07.830
but that because of the attacks on it,

01:16:07.830 --> 01:16:09.423
which are political attacks,

01:16:11.580 --> 01:16:13.190
what would you say?

01:16:13.190 --> 01:16:15.283
Maybe prosperity,

01:16:16.280 --> 01:16:21.210
because enough is a form of
prosperity and or justice,

01:16:21.210 --> 01:16:23.153
maybe justices the word.

01:16:24.420 --> 01:16:28.700
So these, I think are much
more inspiring than adequacy.

01:16:28.700 --> 01:16:32.360
And I thank the questioner
for pointing that out,

01:16:32.360 --> 01:16:36.890
and it is a word game because
it's the discursive battle.

01:16:36.890 --> 01:16:39.683
So, let's think about justice and utopia.

01:16:41.240 --> 01:16:43.080
- Yeah, that's great.

01:16:43.080 --> 01:16:44.920
Actually, there's one burning question

01:16:44.920 --> 01:16:47.570
that I think is so important
that I'm really tempted

01:16:48.678 --> 01:16:49.660
if you don't mind.

01:16:49.660 --> 01:16:52.530
So, there is a view
sometimes held by indigenous

01:16:52.530 --> 01:16:54.520
and post-colonial discourse

01:16:54.520 --> 01:16:56.980
that the entire discourse
of the Anthropocene

01:16:56.980 --> 01:16:59.630
is of sort of white panic.

01:16:59.630 --> 01:17:02.160
Privileged white people who
for the first time realized

01:17:02.160 --> 01:17:04.880
that their ascendancy in
life is being threatened.

01:17:04.880 --> 01:17:06.820
It echoes a bit of what you said

01:17:06.820 --> 01:17:09.320
about the stereotype of geoengineering.

01:17:09.320 --> 01:17:10.733
Can you respond to that?

01:17:12.200 --> 01:17:13.733
- Well, I can see that.

01:17:14.810 --> 01:17:17.610
What I would say is that the Anthropocene

01:17:17.610 --> 01:17:21.860
is a word thought up by
scientist to wave the red flag,

01:17:21.860 --> 01:17:25.090
to pull the emergency alarm
on the wall in the hotel

01:17:25.090 --> 01:17:28.370
and go off like a fire
alarm and shock everybody.

01:17:28.370 --> 01:17:31.000
We're in an era where human beings

01:17:31.000 --> 01:17:34.200
have a over determining
effect on the biosphere

01:17:34.200 --> 01:17:35.830
that can be destructive.

01:17:35.830 --> 01:17:37.170
And so, we're in the business of

01:17:37.170 --> 01:17:41.360
global planetary biospheric management

01:17:41.360 --> 01:17:46.020
or finessing or cooperation.

01:17:46.020 --> 01:17:48.650
Management sounds bad, but cooperation.

01:17:48.650 --> 01:17:50.260
I mean, we need to get along

01:17:50.260 --> 01:17:53.230
in our biosphere for the long haul.

01:17:53.230 --> 01:17:56.830
So, there's been so much argument

01:17:56.830 --> 01:17:58.830
about what the Anthropocene means

01:17:58.830 --> 01:18:01.953
that I think that this
also can be included.

01:18:03.070 --> 01:18:04.670
Some people date the Anthropocene

01:18:04.670 --> 01:18:07.840
to simply the arrival
of humans out of Africa.

01:18:07.840 --> 01:18:11.000
People got to Australia
and by the use of fire,

01:18:11.000 --> 01:18:13.610
they changed the biosphere,

01:18:13.610 --> 01:18:15.259
the biomes of Australia by fire

01:18:15.259 --> 01:18:18.750
within a thousand years of arrival.

01:18:18.750 --> 01:18:21.280
In other words, the whole
history of humanity,

01:18:21.280 --> 01:18:23.290
including indigenous people

01:18:23.290 --> 01:18:24.840
has been involved with

01:18:28.140 --> 01:18:31.290
cooperating with the
natural productive forces

01:18:31.290 --> 01:18:33.180
of the planet, the growth,

01:18:33.180 --> 01:18:35.790
such that the Amazon for instance,

01:18:35.790 --> 01:18:39.440
was a gigantic park with islands of

01:18:40.960 --> 01:18:45.600
heavily nitrogenated highland gardens.

01:18:45.600 --> 01:18:46.560
In other words,

01:18:46.560 --> 01:18:49.510
the native population of the Amazon basin

01:18:49.510 --> 01:18:54.130
was in good cooperative relations

01:18:54.130 --> 01:18:59.130
with that very vibrant and
quickly growing landscape,

01:19:01.360 --> 01:19:02.680
difficult landscape for humans.

01:19:02.680 --> 01:19:04.620
And yet humans had it in hand

01:19:04.620 --> 01:19:06.810
and were living with it cooperatively

01:19:06.810 --> 01:19:08.430
for thousands of years.

01:19:08.430 --> 01:19:11.970
So, the indigenous ought
to always be remembered

01:19:12.840 --> 01:19:16.320
and they are not distinct
from the Anthropocene.

01:19:16.320 --> 01:19:19.210
And there's good reasons for white panic.

01:19:19.210 --> 01:19:21.850
I mean, white people
are actually a minority

01:19:21.850 --> 01:19:23.070
in the world's population,

01:19:23.070 --> 01:19:27.930
and they had a moment of
triumphalism and of white supremacy

01:19:27.930 --> 01:19:29.670
and all of the worst kind of ways.

01:19:29.670 --> 01:19:32.220
And that's gonna go away demographically

01:19:32.220 --> 01:19:34.033
and politically and spiritually,

01:19:35.160 --> 01:19:37.480
but you know what, to get back.

01:19:37.480 --> 01:19:39.720
I mean, as an aging white man,

01:19:39.720 --> 01:19:41.590
I can just say it's more comfortable

01:19:41.590 --> 01:19:45.253
just to be one of the group
than to be anything special.

01:19:47.660 --> 01:19:50.010
- I guess, in a cosmic sense, right?

01:19:50.010 --> 01:19:54.343
We are all simultaneously
significant and insignificant.

01:19:55.760 --> 01:19:56.593
Anyway, Stan,

01:19:56.593 --> 01:19:59.020
thank you so much for this wonderful talk

01:19:59.020 --> 01:20:02.490
and for taking the time
to answer these questions.

01:20:02.490 --> 01:20:05.240
We were absolutely
delighted to have you speak

01:20:05.240 --> 01:20:07.460
at the Franke program today.

01:20:07.460 --> 01:20:08.293
- Thank you.

01:20:08.293 --> 01:20:09.126
My pleasure.

01:20:09.126 --> 01:20:09.959
And I wanna say,

01:20:09.959 --> 01:20:12.350
I will have the chats forwarded to me

01:20:12.350 --> 01:20:14.830
so that I can actually see
what people have been saying.

01:20:14.830 --> 01:20:17.640
I haven't been looking in order to focus

01:20:17.640 --> 01:20:19.540
on what we're talking,

01:20:19.540 --> 01:20:21.760
but I'm interested to
read what people said,

01:20:21.760 --> 01:20:25.180
and I want to thank you Priya
for the idea of having me

01:20:25.180 --> 01:20:26.542
and for everybody else involved.

01:20:26.542 --> 01:20:30.220
It's a great pleasure.

01:20:30.220 --> 01:20:31.426
- Thank you very much.

01:20:31.426 --> 01:20:34.310
And thanks everyone for joining in

01:20:34.310 --> 01:20:37.110
and making this a vibrant event.

01:20:37.110 --> 01:20:39.663
Bye, and see you all later.

